<section id="language">
<h2>Read the language</h2>
<p>Use one naming-scope construct outside the model. <code>namespace</code> and <code>package</code> are alternative keyword candidates, not two required layers. The examples provisionally use <code>namespace</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos.Lending;

model LendingRules {
  module Returns {
    fn remaining(outstanding: Int, returned: Int) -&gt; Int
  }
}</code></pre>
<details data-reference="language-names"><summary>Enclosing scopes, nested blocks and qualified modules</summary>
<p><strong>Correction to draft 0.12:</strong> the maintainer meant package or namespace as alternative ways to declare the enclosing naming scope. Draft 0.12 incorrectly introduced both and put them inside model. Those choices and the multi-package source machinery inferred from them are withdrawn. This draft recommends namespace as the spelling and places the scope outside model; keyword choice remains open.</p>
<p><strong>Nested form:</strong> this is equivalent to the line-level namespace example above.</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos {
  namespace Lending {
    model LendingRules {
      module Returns {
        fn remaining(outstanding: Int, returned: Int) -&gt; Int
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>A dotted block, <code>namespace Finos.Lending { ... }</code>, supplies the same naming prefix. If package is selected as the source keyword instead, it replaces namespace in these examples; it does not add another scope around it.</p>
<p><strong>Functional module alternative:</strong> a file may directly declare a qualified module without a model wrapper.</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">module Finos.Lending.LendingRules;

type alias Quantity = Int
fn remaining(outstanding: Quantity, returned: Quantity) -&gt; Quantity</code></pre>
<p>This module declaration is equivalent to the following namespace-plus-module form, following the direction illustrated by <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fsharp/language-reference/namespaces">F# namespaces and qualified modules</a>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos.Lending;

module LendingRules {
  type alias Quantity = Int
  fn remaining(outstanding: Quantity, returned: Quantity) -&gt; Quantity
}</code></pre>
<p>The module form and the explicit model form are both valid authoring directions. They are not automatically equivalent declarations: <code>model LendingRules { module Returns { ... } }</code> declares a model and a Returns module, while <code>module Finos.Lending.LendingRules</code> declares a LendingRules module. The source does not need a model wrapper merely to define functional types and signatures.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Concept</th><th scope="col">Proposed role</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Enclosing scope</td><td>Qualifies the names of models and modules and reduces naming collisions. Choose namespace or package as its spelling. It does not implicitly define a distribution boundary, dependency or visibility grant.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model declaration</td><td>Names the broader artifact and groups its intent, metadata, contracts and modules. In <code>namespace Finos.Lending; model LendingRules { ... }</code>, its qualified source name is <code>Finos.Lending.LendingRules</code>. The existing artifact identity/revision scheme still applies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Module declaration</td><td>Groups functional declarations and may use a qualified path directly. A module source is compiled into a Morphir model using the explicit build/manifest context even when no model wrapper appears.</td></tr>
<tr><td>IR package</td><td>Remains an ownership/dependency component of FQName and the distribution. Keep its identity and version in explicit build/manifest context; do not infer it by splitting a dotted source name.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Proposed scoping rules:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Namespace headers select the scope for following sibling declarations until another namespace header or the enclosing namespace block/file ends. Repeated headers are siblings relative to that lexical base: <code>namespace Loans;</code> then <code>namespace Accounting;</code> does not create Loans.Accounting.</li>
<li>Namespace blocks extend the active naming prefix and restore it on exit. Root namespace headers start from the empty source namespace, independent of an IR package supplied by the build. Nested block and dotted forms resolve identically.</li>
<li>A module header selects the module for subsequent declarations; a sibling module or namespace header ends that region. A module block has an explicit brace boundary. Module names at the file root are fully qualified; within a namespace they are relative to that namespace. Exact absolute-reference and header-terminator punctuation remains open; semicolons here are proposed notation.</li>
<li>Use namespaces outside explicit model/module blocks in the proposed preview. A model may contain modules and scoped conceptual entities. Namespace switches inside a model, models inside modules, and nested model artifacts are not required by this request; introducing them would need a separate use case.</li>
<li>Imports have lexical scope and do not leak across sibling header regions. Resolve expression names through binders, current-module declarations and explicit imports. Resolve conceptual links through their owning and enclosing conceptual scopes, then imports, checking target kind and visibility. Ambiguity is an error; no implicit parent-module import is assumed.</li>
<li>Namespace/module contributions can be reopened across files in the same declared compilation unit. Merge disjoint declarations deterministically while retaining import environments. Duplicate symbols within their declaration category, conflicting model-level properties and normalized-name collisions fail. An algebraic type and its constructor may still share a spelling.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Proposed lowering:</strong> retain the qualified module path as ModuleName, the declared build package as PackageName, and each declaration's local name as Name. An enclosing model name does not silently become another module-path segment. Thus Returns in the first example has module path <code>Finos.Lending.Returns</code>; the qualified-module example has module path <code>Finos.Lending.LendingRules</code>. Keep source scopes and model names in the conceptual graph, with explicit identity mappings for contracts, traits and evidence. Dotted source syntax does not replace canonical IR name serialization.</p>
<p>Changing a source namespace does not change compilation package ownership. A build needing several packages must declare those compilation units and dependencies explicitly; this scope feature does not introduce multi-primary-package distributions. A source/manifest conflict or unresolved output model context produces a diagnostic rather than an inferred package split.</p>
<p><strong>Design rationale and remaining choice:</strong> putting the scope outside model gives model and module declarations a consistent qualified identity and matches the requested source organization. There is no demonstrated need for the opposite nesting. Namespace is recommended because it describes naming without suggesting dependency packaging. Keep model as an explicit option for richer artifact-level declarations; whether it becomes optional sugar in more cases, or is largely replaced by manifest metadata, remains open. A module declaration must not discard required model metadata merely because it is more concise.</p>
</details>
<p>One file can define several modules. Imports reference module identities, independently of where their source lives. Proposed block form:</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos.Lending;

module Types {
  type alias Quantity = Int
}

module Returns {
  import Finos.Lending.Types as Types;

  fn remaining(outstanding: Types.Quantity, returned: Types.Quantity) -&gt; Int
}</code></pre>
<details data-reference="language-imports"><summary>Multiple modules, imports and the role of include</summary>
<p>Multiple modules per source file and declaration reuse are maintainer requirements. The following import spelling, resolution rules and choice to defer include are assistant proposals. File layout must not determine module identity or force one file per module.</p>
<p><strong>Successive module headers:</strong> this is equivalent to the two blocks above.</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos.Lending;

module Types;
type alias Quantity = Int

module Returns;
import Finos.Lending.Types as Types;
fn remaining(outstanding: Types.Quantity, returned: Types.Quantity) -&gt; Int</code></pre>
<p>The second module header ends Types and starts its sibling Returns. It does not nest Returns inside Types. Modules may also be siblings inside an explicit model declaration. The existing nested-block and scope-restoration rules continue to apply.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Need</th><th scope="col">Proposed mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Qualified module access</td><td><code>import Finos.Lending.Types as Types;</code> binds a local alias. References such as <code>Types.Quantity</code> resolve to the original declaration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selected short names</td><td><code>import Finos.Lending.Types exposing (Quantity);</code> brings only the named public declarations into the lexical environment. Importing a type does not automatically expose its constructors.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Another package</td><td><code>import sdk::List as List;</code> uses an explicit manifest dependency alias. The alias supplies package identity/version; the path identifies a module within it. Exact separator spelling is provisional.</td></tr>
<tr><td>More source files</td><td>The manifest/build input lists source files for the compilation unit. Adding a file does not inject its text into another file or inherit that file's active namespace/imports.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Module composition</td><td>A future include could compose or re-export declarations, with explicit conflict, visibility, identity and contract rules. Ordinary imports do not perform that merge. No language-level include is required in the proposed preview.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Resolution and scope:</strong> collect declarations from the complete supplied source set before resolving imports and references. Textual ordering of module blocks or source files does not control availability; forward imports between same-unit modules are valid. Unprefixed module import paths are fully qualified within the current compilation package, even inside a namespace. They are not silently prefixed with the current namespace. Cross-package imports require an explicit dependency alias and a declared dependency; no search through arbitrary packages or file paths.</p>
<p>An import affects following declarations in its lexical region and nested scopes. Imports declared in an enclosing namespace/model region are inherited; imports local to a module or contribution do not leak into sibling modules or another file's contribution. Explicit local binders can shadow outer names under the existing lexical rules. Conflicting imports or imported names colliding with declarations in the same scope produce diagnostics rather than file-order-dependent selection. No wildcard import or implicit re-export is introduced. An alias never changes a declaration's FQName, opaque identity, ownership or source links.</p>
<p>Import visibility follows the declaration's exports. It cannot reveal private constructors or opaque representations, bypass dependency boundaries, or make an unimplemented function executable. The example Quantity alias is illustrative; the module's export configuration must make it public when imported. Exact export notation remains part of the broader module design. Resolved references retain links to contracts, type refinements, source revisions and metadata in the dependency model.</p>
<p><strong>Source and cycle behavior:</strong> every source file is parsed once in its own declared lexical context, with normalized source identity preventing duplicate inclusion through build inputs. Import resolution may revisit a module without redeclaring it. Dependency cycles between modules are not inherently value recursion: collect signatures together and permit the guarded recursive type cases already supported by the type profile. Reject unproductive alias cycles, invalid initialization/evaluation cycles and any unsupported semantic dependency explicitly. Future implementation evaluation does not acquire textual include order. Artifact/package dependency-cycle policy remains a separate build concern.</p>
<p><strong>Why import first:</strong> it makes the intended module dependency visible while allowing files to be split or combined without changing resolved names. Textual inclusion would add order and scope effects that this design does not need. If users need reusable fragments that merge into a module or interface, define include as a separate composition feature instead of making it a synonym for import. This is a proposed first-delivery boundary, not a rejection of future composition.</p>
</details>
<p>These type sketches assume a module and its explicit build context. Refinement and contract punctuation remain proposals.</p><pre><code class="language-moonbit">type alias Quantity = Int where self &gt;= 0
opaque type alias LoanId = String
type alias Quad a b c d = (a, b, c, d)

type ReturnError = OverReturn
type Status = | Pending | Returned Quantity
type alias Identifier = String | Int

fn acceptReturn(loan: Loan, returned: Quantity)
  -&gt; Result ReturnError Loan</code></pre><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Form</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>type alias A = B</code></td><td>A transparent name for an existing type.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>opaque type alias A = B</code></td><td>Distinct identity; the module controls construction and representation access.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>type A = B | C</code></td><td>Named constructors. A leading pipe is also accepted.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>type alias A = B | C</code></td><td>An untagged union of existing types.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Expand a topic below for binding, narrowing, tuple, construction and contract rules.</p>
<p>Multiline text should work wherever a String literal is allowed. Proposed raw form:</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">requirement ReturnBalance {
  id: "LENDING-RETURN-001"
  text:
    #|When a return is accepted,
    #|the lending system shall reduce the outstanding quantity
    #|by the accepted quantity.
    #|
    #|The loan's identity and asset must remain unchanged.
}</code></pre>
<details data-reference="language-text"><summary>Multiline strings: whitespace, literal content and model preservation</summary>
<p>Multiline text is a maintainer requirement. The following lexical rules are a design proposal inspired by MoonBit. They apply to intent, requirements, descriptions, metadata and String-valued examples wherever the surrounding grammar accepts a String literal. A text block does not introduce a new requirement kind or make its prose executable.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Proposed behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Line prefix</td><td>Consecutive source lines beginning with optional indentation followed by <code>#|</code> form one literal. Indentation before the marker is syntax; everything after it is content, including spaces and tabs. No automatic dedent or optional padding space is removed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Newlines</td><td>Join payloads with LF. Accept LF and CRLF source line endings, producing the same value. Do not add an implicit opening or trailing newline. An empty prefixed line contributes an empty payload; a final empty prefixed line adds a trailing LF.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Literal content</td><td>Quotes, backslashes, braces, HTML, Markdown and Gherkin remain ordinary text. Escape sequences and interpolation are not evaluated. A marker or comment token after the initial marker is content.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Block boundary</td><td>The first line without the prefix ends the literal. A blank line inside the value must carry its own <code>#|</code>. A bare blank line or comment cannot silently join two blocks. Normal surrounding syntax resumes on an unprefixed line.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interpolation</td><td>Also accept <code>$|</code> lines with <code>\{expression}</code> holes, under the typed-template rules below. Consecutive <code>#|</code> and <code>$|</code> lines can form one block; each line's prefix controls whether holes are parsed. Literal-content rules apply to raw lines and to text outside holes. The first unprefixed line still ends the block.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">#|first
#|second
#|

// The block above has the String value "first\nsecond\n".
// Spaces after the marker are significant:
#|  indented

// Raw content, including the backslash and braces:
#|"quoted" \n \{name}</code></pre>
<p>Lower the decoded value to the existing String literal representation where supported; this syntax does not require a new IR literal node. Rich model/source metadata retains the original spelling, source spans and line endings when exact source preservation is required. Model-to-source generation may normalize formatting but must preserve the String value, including leading spaces, blank lines and final LF. EARS/Gherkin generation keeps the same requirement links and validation boundaries as other text; embedding their prose alone establishes no executable claim.</p>
</details>
<p>Proposed reusable template form, with rendering deferred until arguments are supplied:</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">template ReturnSummary(loanId: String, returned: Int) {
  $|Loan \{loanId}: accepted \{returned} units.
  #|The loan's identity and asset remain unchanged.
}</code></pre>
<details data-reference="language-templates"><summary>Typed templates: binding, evaluation and interoperability</summary>
<p>The maintainer has raised interpolation as a useful templating capability. The following is an assistant proposal for the preview, replacing draft 0.10's deferral. The <code>template</code> keyword and braces are illustrative; plain <code>fn</code> remains the selected function-specification form.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">Proposed rule</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Value and declaration</td><td>An interpolated expression produces a String when evaluated in a bound environment. A named template retains typed parameters and ordered literal/expression segments until explicitly rendered. Parameter names and source/revision links remain model data. A template is not an already rendered String or an implicitly executed fn body.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lexing</td><td>In a <code>$|</code> payload, only <code>\{</code> begins a hole. All other characters are literal, including backslash-n. Holes contain one single-line expression; the expression parser matches braces and respects quoted strings. No multiline literal may nest inside a hole. Use a raw line for literal hole notation; a hole returning a String can also insert it. Rendered characters are never parsed again as holes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Binding</td><td>Resolve parameters and explicit, immutable model constants lexically; reject unknown names. A declaration with unprovided runtime parameters is valid. Rendering requires well-typed values for every required parameter, with no missing-value-to-empty-string conversion. Record field references retain their resolved field identities. Optional values require an explicit branch or declared default.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Use a versioned, bounded, pure expression profile. Initially reuse supported contract expressions, excluding arbitrary function calls, recursion and host effects. There is no implicit environment, clock, network or LLM call. Rendering failures and limit exhaustion produce diagnostics and no successful artifact.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Formatting</td><td>Initially interpolate String unchanged, Bool as lowercase true/false and exact Int as base-10 digits without grouping or a plus sign. Other types need explicit versioned formatters; do not use host debug output or expose opaque representations. This first renderer is a subset of the eventual template representation, not a restriction on what imported AI metadata can retain.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target output</td><td>Interpolation renders plain text. An HTML, JSON or Gherkin generator owns context-appropriate encoding and syntax validation. Rendering prose does not evaluate a business contract, establish a claim or invoke a tool.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Retain the template AST, original dialect/spelling, argument declarations, referenced constants and source links in the rich model. Rendering records template/model revision, explicit arguments, formatter/evaluator profile and output digest. Constant folding or lowering to existing String operations may produce derived executable IR; it cannot replace the authored template. A bare IR export reports loss if required template information has nowhere to go.</p>
<p>Smithy-compatible prompt templates use a separate, identified placeholder dialect. Their raw text can be carried in <code>#|</code> lines without eager expansion. Translate to Morphir parameter-reference segments only where binding and formatting semantics are defined; preserve the original dialect on round-trip. A Morphir arithmetic expression in a hole is not automatically exportable as a Smithy placeholder. Diagnose the gap, or explicitly render a concrete artifact with provenance when that is what the caller requests.</p>
</details>
<details data-reference="language-0"><summary>Full language overview and rental sketch</summary>
<p>Provide a Morphir-specific textual frontend, provisionally called <code>morphir-spec</code>, with an explicit draft language version. Its type declarations follow the maintainer's ML-family direction and restored Elm-style distinction: <code>type alias</code> introduces a type expression, including a record, and <code>type</code> introduces constructor alternatives. <code>opaque</code> provides the abstraction boundary described below. F#, OCaml, Elm, Gleam and other functional languages remain inputs to the final design. The opaque-alias and <code>where</code> forms are proposed Morphir extensions, not claimed Elm syntax.</p>
<p>The semantic distinctions below are part of this draft. Plain <code>fn</code> is the selected starting function-specification form. Remaining delimiter choices and parser implementation are delegated to the implementation plan. A preview source extension can be chosen without committing the eventual full language to a specification/implementation file split. The examples are notation sketches, not currently accepted source. Intent and requirement notation may have its own structured forms; that does not require domain types to use a document-schema style.</p>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">namespace Finos.Examples.Lending;

model RentalRules {
  intent TrackReturns {
    text: "Track what borrowers still owe after accepted returns."
  }

  requirement ReturnBalance {
    id: "LENDING-RETURN-001"
    kind: functional
    refines: TrackReturns
    ears: event {
      when: "a return is accepted"
      system: "the lending system"
      shall: "reduce the outstanding quantity by the accepted quantity"
    }
  }

  module Rentals {
    type alias Quantity = Int where self &gt;= 0

    type alias Loan =
      { id : String
      , asset : String
      , outstanding : Quantity
      }

    type ReturnError
      = OverReturn

    fn acceptReturn(loan: Loan, returned: Quantity)
      -&gt; Result ReturnError Loan
    // Contracts refer directly to loan, returned and result.
    // Identified clauses define success, rejection and preserved fields.
  }
}
</code></pre>
<table><thead><tr><th>Functional type construct</th><th>Proposed notation / role</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Transparent alias</td><td><code>type alias Label = String</code>; a reusable name for a type expression.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opaque alias / newtype</td><td><code>opaque type alias LoanId = String</code>; distinct type identity with a module-private representation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record and tuple aliases</td><td><code>type alias Person = { name : String, age : Int }</code> and <code>type alias NamedAge = (String, Int)</code>; names for immutable structural product types.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opaque record</td><td><code>opaque type alias Account = { id : String, balance : Int }</code>; distinct type with fields accessible only inside its defining module.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Algebraic data type</td><td><code>type Status = Pending | Returned Quantity</code>; named alternatives with typed constructor payloads and exhaustive matching.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Untagged union</td><td><code>type alias Identifier = String | Int</code>; values belonging to either existing type, without new constructors.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Type parameters</td><td><code>type Decision error value = Rejected error | Accepted value</code>; explicit parametric type constructors, independent of metadata.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explicit wrapper</td><td><code>type LoanId = LoanId String</code>; an alternative declaration using a named constructor, whose visibility follows module exports.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Function specification</td><td><code>fn acceptReturn(loan: Loan, returned: Quantity) -&gt; Result ReturnError Loan</code>; named inputs and a result type, with no implementation body required.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The alias keyword resolves the single-constructor ambiguity: <code>type alias A = B</code> names an existing type B, while <code>type A = B</code> introduces a nominal type with a nullary constructor B. Subsequent constructors use <code>|</code>; a leading pipe before the first constructor is accepted and optional. Thus <code>type A = B</code> and <code>type A = | B</code> have identical meaning. In a type expression, <code>|</code> combines existing types into an untagged union. A leading pipe is optional for constructor lists only. Resolve names according to the declaration form, without guessing from names currently in scope. An unresolved B in an alias produces an unresolved-type diagnostic.</p>
<p><code>opaque</code> requires nominal identity and hides the representation across the defining module boundary. Applied to <code>type alias</code>, including a record alias, it creates a newtype. Applied to <code>type</code>, it hides the constructors of an already nominal algebraic data type, without adding another wrapper. A transparent alias to an existing opaque type preserves that type's identity and visibility; it does not reveal its representation or create a second newtype.</p>
<p>The early self-specification profile must accept transparent and opaque aliases, records, tuples and parameterized algebraic declarations with explicit annotations, including mutually recursive algebraic definitions and the finite collection types needed by the pinned IR. Resolve parameter arity and constructor payload types. Reject unproductive alias cycles; allow recursion guarded by algebraic constructors, and check supplied finite values under explicit depth/node limits. This replaces the earlier blanket deferral of recursive declarations. Operations checked by the first evaluator profile have concrete, first-order input/result types. The language can describe nested function types, but checking higher-order values and partial applications is outside that execution profile and reports unsupported when requested. General ML module signatures/functors and inference/generalization from implementation bodies remain later work; explicitly declared polymorphic signatures are included now.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="language-1"><summary>Untagged unions</summary>
<h3>Untagged unions</h3>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">type alias Identifier = String | Int
type alias Scalar = String | Int | Bool
type alias OneOf a b = a | b
type alias Identifiers = List (String | Int)

type IdentifierValue = TextId String | NumericId Int
</code></pre>
<p>Identifier accepts an existing String or Int value without adding a constructor or selecting a named variant. IdentifierValue is the separate tagged form. Union membership means membership in at least one alternative; overlapping alternatives are allowed and do not select a preferred branch. Reordering alternatives changes neither membership nor assignability. Repeated alternatives and nested unions are semantically idempotent/associative, while source order, annotations and refinements remain available for diagnostics. Do not simplify away an annotated/refined alternative and lose its obligations or provenance.</p>
<p>Unions compose with aliases, records, tuples, type parameters and opaque aliases. Generic substitution may make alternatives equal or overlapping. Union notation binds less tightly than type application and more tightly than the function arrow: <code>a -&gt; b | c</code> means <code>a -&gt; (b | c)</code>. Parenthesize union inputs or collection elements. This precedence is an assistant proposal. Tagged constructors retain the existing optional-leading-pipe rule; an alias's alternatives are type expressions.</p>
<p>The initial checker allows a known member type to widen to a union without a runtime conversion. A union can flow to a narrower type only when every possible alternative is assignable to it or a checked type-membership guard establishes the narrower type in that branch. A value must be narrowed before using an operation specific to one alternative. Use explicit signatures and conservative structural/nominal checks; do not introduce solver-based refinement implication, numeric coercions or arbitrary subtyping inference. Opaque members retain their nominal identities and visibility.</p>
<p>The conceptual checker evaluates membership and narrowing for supported, concrete, first-order member types under the same resource limits as other checks. A guard checks the supplied value's type/structure and any required refinement, not a fabricated union tag. If several alternatives accept the same typed value, membership succeeds without choosing an arm. Raw decoding must report ambiguity when alternatives produce different typed interpretations of the same input; require an explicit codec decision instead of taking the first alternative. Unsupported checks are reported explicitly and cannot establish a negative membership result.</p>
<p>Propose <code>Union(TypeAttributes, Array[Type])</code> in the IR 4.0.0 draft, with at least two listed alternatives in an authored union node. Members may become equivalent after substitution; this remains valid. Current MoonBit v4 has no such node. Implementing the node, codecs, traversal/substitution/equality rules and capability detection is an early dependency for full projection and compiled execution, tracked by morphir-ukvn.7.2. The conceptual checker may check supplied union cases before backend support exists; an implementation binding or compiled predicate that needs unsupported union semantics must refuse execution. No implicit lowering to an invented tagged ADT is allowed. Backend-specific lowering needs an explicit representation mapping that preserves observable semantics.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="language-2"><summary>Generics and type variables</summary>
<h3>Generics and type variables</h3>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">type alias Pair a b = (a, b)
type alias Triple a b c = (a, b, c)
type alias Quad a b c d = (a, b, c, d)
type Maybe a = Nothing | Just a
type Result error value = | Err error | Ok value
opaque type alias Id entity = String

fn identity(value: a) -&gt; a
fn map(items: List a, transform: a -&gt; b) -&gt; List b
</code></pre>
<p>Lowercase type variables are scoped binders. A type declaration lists its parameters explicitly; its body may refer only to those parameters and resolved named types. Reject duplicate parameters and unbound variables in type declarations. A standalone function signature implicitly universally quantifies its free type variables over the whole signature, so both occurrences of <code>a</code> in identity refer to the same type. Variables in different declarations have separate scopes; renaming a bound variable preserves meaning. Constructors inherit their parent declaration's parameters. Contract binders share the operation's type-variable scope.</p>
<p>The named-parameter <code>fn</code> examples retain that implicit universal-binding rule. An explicit function binder list, such as <code>fn[a, b]</code>, remains a syntax candidate; the plain-fn decision alone does not select it or change generic semantics.</p>
<p>The initial generic system is rank-1 parametric polymorphism with first-order type parameters: parameters stand for complete types, and named type constructors have checked arity. Support capture-avoiding substitution and fresh instantiation when checking uses. A repeated variable must use one consistent substitution across a signature. For <code>a -&gt; a</code> instantiated at Int, both input and result must be Int. Instantiated at <code>String | Int</code>, the input and result may inhabit different members while both satisfy the same substituted type. Type membership alone does not verify the behavior of identity or prove parametricity of an external binding; preserving the original value needs its own contract. Higher-kinded parameters, higher-rank quantifiers and typeclass-style constraints are later features, not prerequisites for declaring the selected generic SDK APIs.</p>
<p>Opaque types preserve their parameters as part of nominal identity even when a parameter is absent from the representation. Thus <code>Id Loan</code> and <code>Id Borrower</code> are distinct types. The preview introduces no implicit coercions between those applications. IR projection retains parameter binders, Type.Variable occurrences and ordered reference arguments; rich round-trips preserve their relationships. Executing a polymorphic contract case requires a recorded concrete substitution. Testing selected instantiations provides evidence for those instantiations, not a proof for every type. A higher-order SDK signature can be represented and checked structurally while its execution coverage remains explicitly unsupported.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="language-3"><summary>Tuple arity</summary>
<h3>Tuple arity</h3>
<p>Support ordered heterogeneous tuples of any finite arity of two or more, with no language-level maximum. Each declaration fixes its arity and element types, which may contain type variables, nested tuples or other supported types. Tuple types, values and destructuring patterns must agree in arity and element order. A flat four-element tuple remains distinct from a nested pair of pairs. The parser, type checker, contract evaluator, case generator and codecs must preserve that distinction and must not impose a pair/triple-only restriction.</p>
<p>Morphir's existing v4 <code>Tuple(TypeAttributes, Array[Type])</code> supports an ordered list of element types. Project directly to that representation, preserving generic substitutions. Execution limits may bound total input size, but are reported resource limits rather than a fixed tuple-arity rule. A target with a smaller native tuple limit needs an explicit semantics-preserving lowering or an unsupported diagnostic. Grouping parentheses and unit syntax are separate from this requirement for tuples of two or more elements.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="language-4"><summary>Opaque aliases and construction</summary>
<h3>Opaque aliases and construction</h3>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">module Lending {
  opaque type alias LoanId = String
  opaque type alias BorrowerId = String
  opaque type alias PositiveQuantity = Int where self &gt; 0

  fn createQuantity(value: Int) -&gt; Result QuantityError PositiveQuantity
  fn quantityValue(quantity: PositiveQuantity) -&gt; Int
  type QuantityError = MustBePositive
}
</code></pre>
<p>Each opaque declaration has its own identity, resolved by fully qualified name and type arguments. LoanId, BorrowerId and String are incompatible without an explicit conversion, including within the defining module. The defining module can explicitly wrap and unwrap its representation; imported modules cannot use those private operations, pattern-match the representation or inherit its arithmetic/order operations. Public factories and observers are ordinary, explicitly declared functions. No public constructor, cast or accessor is generated automatically. Parameterized opaque aliases follow the same arity and concrete-instantiation rules as other declarations.</p>
<p>A refinement on an opaque alias evaluates <code>self</code> against the underlying representation in the defining module. Construction must establish that predicate; the preview checks it at the construction boundary. A factory can remain a signature with contracts before it has a body. That supports checking supplied candidates but does not make the factory callable. Definition-scoped fixtures may supply a representation tagged with its opaque type identity; the checker validates the representation and refinement before admitting the value. A public consumer needs a bound factory or declared validated codec to import raw data. Unsupported calls remain unsupported; declaring an observer does not add arbitrary function calls to the first predicate profile.</p>
<p>Project the public signature to Morphir's existing <code>OpaqueTypeSpecification</code>. A proposed internal encoding for a newtype uses a single-constructor <code>CustomTypeDefinition</code> with private constructors and retains the source declaration and refinement in the conceptual profile. Opaque variants use their declared constructors with private visibility. A signature-only projection must omit the representation; a complete authoring model may retain it. Opacity is an abstraction boundary, not a claim that the model package conceals sensitive data. Never lower a newtype to a transparent <code>TypeAliasSpecification</code> and lose its identity. Backends may optimize the wrapper while preserving semantics; this proposal promises no universal zero-allocation representation.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="language-5"><summary>Function specifications and future implementations</summary>
<h3>Function specifications and future implementations</h3>
<pre><code class="language-moonbit">fn remaining(outstanding: Quantity, returned: Quantity) -&gt; Quantity
contract {
  requires returned &lt;= outstanding
  ensures result == outstanding - returned
}
</code></pre>
<p>A bodyless <code>fn</code> is a complete function specification. It is neither an executable implementation nor an incomplete-body placeholder. The contract block above is proposed attachment syntax; it contains predicates and does not supply the function body. This illustrative arithmetic helper is separate from acceptReturn, whose Result-based rejection behavior remains unchanged. Preconditions bind the declared input names; postconditions additionally bind <code>result</code>. Reject duplicate parameter names and use of the reserved result binder as an input name in this profile. Clause identities and source links remain required in the resolved contract graph even when omitted from a short sketch.</p>
<p>Project parameter names, order and types directly to Morphir ValueSpecification inputs, and the declared return type to its output. For acceptReturn the input list has two entries, loan and returned. Contracts evaluate after the complete declared input list has been supplied. <code>fn f(x: A, y: B) -&gt; C</code> has two inputs; <code>fn f(pair: (A, B)) -&gt; C</code> has one tuple input. A function-valued result remains the output type, not an extra input. Arrow notation remains available for function type expressions. Currying, partial application, call-site labels and the full language's callable-type representation need explicit semantics; comma-separated parameters alone do not settle them. The first preview does not execute partial applications or higher-order values.</p>
<p>A later full-language profile may distinguish specifications and definitions through optional bodies, <code>declare fn</code>, separate file extensions or another marker. Choose this when implementation syntax is designed. Preserve the meaning of existing specifications through an explicit language-version/migration rule; a contract block must not silently become a function implementation. Named parameter binders do not automatically make names into call-site labels.</p>
<p>Required authoring constructs for the preview: model/package identity, module, prose intent/concept, identified requirement with extensible kind, explicit source/relationship references, structured event-style EARS clauses, supported type declarations/refinements, operation signature, identified contract clauses, and typed example cases with expected observations. Express unresolved concepts explicitly. A general library/application implementation language is not part of this frontend's first profile.</p>
<p>Structured EARS authoring and deterministic rendering are in the first delivery. Arbitrary raw EARS sentences can be retained as source text with an unresolved interpretation; automatically extracting every EARS pattern from English is later work. Other EARS patterns remain documented portfolio cases. A Where feature condition must not silently become a runtime guard, and a While state condition must not automatically become a function precondition.</p>
<p>Do not route rich conceptual models through the current Scheme spec printer: its documented annotation losses make it unsuitable for this role. Reuse appropriate naming/type/evaluator facilities while preserving all profile data through the new frontend and the existing rich model boundary.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="smithy">
<h2>Smithy coverage</h2>
<p><strong>Required direction:</strong> Morphir must represent these four Smithy capability families. The table identifies design obligations and current gaps; it does not claim implemented parity.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Smithy capability</th><th scope="col">Morphir model needs</th><th scope="col">Qualification still needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Contract traits</td><td>Named conditions, documentation, target bindings and explicit expression semantics.</td><td>JMESPath mapping and behavior fixtures.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RPC v2 JSON</td><td>Service/operation exposure, JSON wire rules and protocol metadata.</td><td>Shape coverage and client/server wire conformance.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RPC v2 CBOR</td><td>Independent CBOR wire profile, numeric/tag rules and streaming declarations.</td><td>Protocol-specific coverage and diagnostics.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI traits</td><td>Named prompts, descriptions, argument types, templates and selection guidance.</td><td>Binding, template interchange and generated prompt validation.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Functional modules own types and functions. Service bindings expose selected functions as operations. Versioned traits attach contracts, protocols and AI context to identified constructs.</p>
<details data-reference="smithy-contracts"><summary>Contract and AI trait coverage</summary>
<p>The inspected <a href="https://smithy.io/2.0/additional-specs/contract-traits.html">contract specification</a> defines <code>conditions</code> as named JMESPath predicates with required documentation, applicable to shapes other than services, operations and resources. Conditions validate values; they should not alter generated signatures or run automatically during serialization. Morphir must preserve these target and enforcement semantics when importing them.</p>
<p>Proposed condition representation: identity/name, subject reference, documentation, expression source, expression language/version, data-view profile and expected Boolean result. Retain the Smithy source trait ID and provenance. Native predicates may be derived only with an explicit equivalence mapping. Otherwise keep a structured external-expression node that can be checked by a qualified adapter. Keeping an opaque string alone is preservation, not semantic coverage.</p>
<p>Full compatibility is an acceptance target beyond the first scalar checker. Inventory JMESPath operators/functions and qualify selection, collection operations, null/missing values, Boolean outcomes and error behavior against independent fixtures. Pin the <a href="https://smithy.io/2.0/additional-specs/waiters.html#jmespath-data-model">Smithy JMESPath data view</a>, including numeric conversion and shape-member naming. Do not feed protocol JSON into JMESPath by assumption or silently substitute Morphir exact arithmetic for a different numeric model. Unsupported semantics remain a reported gap. Morphir's operation pre/postconditions remain available independently.</p>
<p>The inspected <a href="https://smithy.io/2.0/additional-specs/ai-traits.html">AI specification</a> defines <code>smithy.ai#prompts</code> on services and operations: named entries with description, template, optional arguments structure reference and optional <code>preferWhen</code>. Its placeholders use <code>{{parameterName}}</code>. Preserve all these fields and service/operation attachment scope. Its prompt metadata can feed MCP prompt generation.</p>
<p>Proposed prompt representation adds an identified typed template reference and requirement/intent links to the preserved trait data. Keep <code>preferWhen</code> as descriptive guidance unless a separate formal predicate is explicitly authored. A workflow described by prompt prose does not become an executable workflow. Imported optional argument declarations and unresolved bindings remain inspectable; render-time requirements are checked before expansion. Any unspecified foreign formatting behavior must be named as a Morphir rendering policy, not claimed as Smithy-defined semantics.</p>
</details>
<details data-reference="smithy-protocols"><summary>Protocol profiles and functional service bindings</summary>
<p>Use the precise protocol identities <code>smithy.protocols#rpcv2Json</code> and <code>smithy.protocols#rpcv2Cbor</code>. Smithy RPC v2 JSON is distinct from the separate JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol.</p>
<p>Both specify HTTP RPC, ordered HTTP/event-stream versions, routing, headers, modeled errors and event streams. Cover those alongside CORS, endpoint/host-label, compression and error-status traits. A valid JSON or CBOR payload alone does not establish protocol conformance. Sources: <a href="https://smithy.io/2.0/additional-specs/protocols/smithy-rpc-v2-json.html">RPC v2 JSON</a> and <a href="https://smithy.io/2.0/additional-specs/protocols/smithy-rpc-v2-cbor.html">RPC v2 CBOR</a>.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Profile</th><th scope="col">Specific distinctions to retain</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>JSON</td><td>Base64 blobs; strings for arbitrary-precision integers/decimals; special floating-point values; epoch timestamps; document values; member-tagged unions; omitted/default values; unit bodies and modeled errors.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBOR</td><td>Byte strings; bignum and decimal tags; tagged epoch timestamps; numeric-width rules; undefined/null handling; no document support in the inspected protocol. Service routing compatibility also differs from JSON.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Proposed service binding records service identity/version, operation identities, function references, explicit input-record-to-parameter mapping, output/error mapping and protocol applications. A service may select functions from several modules; a module need not expose a service. Preserve external shape/member IDs through renames. Remote side effects and streaming belong to the service adapter, not an implicit effect on a pure domain function. Operations may remain unimplemented declarations.</p>
<p>The representation must have a typed path for the complete relevant Smithy shape closure, including numeric widths/precision, decimals, blobs, timestamps, documents where allowed, enums, lists/maps, records, tagged unions, optional/required/default members and streaming shapes. Library-defined types and versioned semantic profiles may supply these; the initial Int/String execution subset is insufficient evidence of parity. Preserve unknown-value and evolution behavior. Smithy unions retain member tags; they do not map automatically to Morphir untagged unions. Refuse an untagged-union wire mapping without an explicit unambiguous encoding.</p>
<p>Keep wire semantics separate from domain refinements and from rich-model Ion transport. A protocol application references versioned encoding/behavior rules and explicit supported-trait semantics. Retain client/server distinctions and normative requirement levels. Validate unsupported shape/protocol combinations before generation. Test request/response framing, errors, defaults, omission, precision and event streams for each protocol. Reuse pinned upstream protocol-test models where available and add independent cases for uncovered rules. No live RPC server/client is implied by storing a service binding.</p>
</details>
<details data-reference="smithy-qualification"><summary>Coverage gate, delivery stages and typed extensions</summary>
<p>Define traits as versioned typed declarations with stable namespaced identities, admissible target kinds, payload schemas, reference resolution and semantic capability requirements. Preserve applications on models, modules, types, members, functions/operations and service bindings as appropriate. These are proposed Morphir target kinds; imported Smithy traits retain their own applicability rules. Unknown descriptive applications can round-trip; a required semantic application cannot be silently ignored.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Evidence required</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Represent</td><td>Every pinned source feature maps to an authorable typed construct, semantic rule or explicit missing-feature issue. DSL/model round-trips retain data and meaning. Generic metadata storage alone does not close a row.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validate and exchange</td><td>Trait targets, references, bindings and profile constraints validate. Import/export preserves supported semantics, with explicit gaps for others.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execute or generate</td><td>Condition/template evaluators and protocol generators pass profile-specific fixtures; runtime adapters pass their wire tests independently.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The planning gate is an exhaustive, version-pinned inventory of the four specifications and their transitive shape/trait dependencies. Each row records source section/revision or content digest, Morphir construct, represent/validate/import/export/execute/generate status, test fixture and owning issue. No row disappears because a preview cannot run it. Overall parity requires complete applicable coverage; draft 0.11 supplies a design-level matrix, not that completed inventory.</p>
<p>Proposed first-preview additions are raw/interpolated text, bounded typed templates, authorable typed extension/service/prompt/foreign-condition records and representative preservation/validation fixtures for all four families. The exhaustive mapping gate must identify any core model gaps before freezing the language profile. Full JMESPath execution, protocol runtimes and broad Smithy import/export are separately qualified follow-ons. This sequencing preserves early IR/SDK dogfooding while treating full representability as a required destination, not an optional domain example.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="self-specification">
<h2>Morphir specifies itself</h2>
<p><strong>Primary early use case:</strong> Morphir defines its IR and SDK, then checks implementations against those specifications.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Deliverable</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 · Bootstrap</td><td>Pinned source authority, toolchain and independent fixtures.</td><td>The candidate compiler is not its only judge.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 · First slice</td><td>Dependency-closed v4 IR Type model; SDK integer add, subtract and abs.</td><td>Recursive/collection checks, malformed wire cases, native SDK bindings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3 · Compare</td><td>Run model-owned checks alongside selected existing schema gates.</td><td>Resolve disagreements against reviewed versioned sources.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4 · Replace</td><td>Remove each covered JSON Schema dependency.</td><td>A coverage manifest names every remaining gate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">The initial slice does not retire all schema gates. Full coverage of the gates being replaced is the next early milestone.</div>
<details data-reference="self-specification-0"><summary>Specification layers and first-slice rules</summary>
<p>This is a primary early use case. Publish versioned Morphir models for the IR and SDK in finos/morphir, with executable checks consumed by morphir-moonbit and the shared compatibility kit. The intended authority is the reviewed Morphir specification, including its encoding profiles and contracts. JSON Schema becomes unnecessary for the migrated compatibility/verification path. JSON remains a supported representation; optional schema exports for external tools are derived artifacts.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>What it defines</th><th>Evidence required</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>IR semantic model</td><td>Names, types, values, patterns, modules, packages and model relationships; parameters, recursion, visibility and invariants.</td><td>Validate supplied model instances against the selected revision, including malformed nested values and broken identities. Structural validity and well-typedness are separate reported checks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>IR encoding profile</td><td>Version envelopes, constructor tags, payload positions, required/optional fields, collection encodings and unknown-data policy for a selected wire format.</td><td>Validate raw input before decoding can discard distinctions; test codec round-trips and malformed wire fixtures. Record duplicate-key, unexpected-field and wrong-arity policies explicitly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SDK module specifications</td><td>Public types/signatures, operation contracts, error behavior and laws with declared applicability.</td><td>Execute selected cases against actual SDK bindings and record operation/profile/version coverage. Signature compatibility alone does not establish behavioral conformance.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The first dogfooding slice is a dependency-closed IR Type model plus the integer specializations of SDK Basics add, subtract and abs. The Type model must include every constructor and its actual dependencies in the pinned version, including recursive children, attributes and collection representations. Use current v4 as the initial proposed version, with explicit source revision and no claim to cover all historical formats. Publish the module/constructor/rule coverage manifest alongside the fixture set. Full Type/Value/Pattern/module/package coverage and the remaining supported encoding/version gates form the next early milestone, before claiming the compatibility kit has abandoned JSON Schema.</p>
<p>Finite list/map structure validation and bounded traversal of recursive values are required in this early profile. Structural recursion belongs to the checker, not unrestricted user-authored recursion. Cross-reference/type-arity checks use declared, versioned validation rules with evidence linked to their requirements. An as-yet unsupported rule must appear as an uncovered obligation; no hard-coded rule may silently stand in for a complete executable specification. Arbitrary quantified business predicates, higher-order SDK execution and a general theorem prover remain outside the initial profile.</p>
<p><strong>Proposed execution mechanism:</strong> encoding and reference rules are executable typed declarations in the model's versioned validation profile, not just names of handwritten validators. Encoding descriptors specify tags, payload counts, field presence, duplicate/unknown-field policy and scalar lexical constraints. Rules compose a closed set of structural operations: traverse supplied finite children, require unique keys, resolve a named reference in an explicit environment, and check type-argument arity. The bootstrap checker interprets these declarations through versioned intrinsics with specified behavior and independent conformance fixtures. Handwritten intrinsic implementations are permitted; an arbitrary host callback is not an authored rule. Raw parsing retains key occurrences and number lexemes until the applicable descriptors have checked them. Each failure reports its rule identity and source path. These bounded structural operations extend the self-specification profile without adding unrestricted recursive functions or unbounded quantification to the contract language.</p>
<p>The MoonBit SDK bindings are native functions, so qualify a small explicit adapter for the selected operations alongside the existing IR evaluator adapter. Pin the FQName-to-binding mapping, concrete integer semantics and implementation revision. For example, abs must return the input when nonnegative and its negation otherwise; fixtures include zero, both signs and integers beyond machine width. Keep hand-authored expected results and deliberate mutants. An oracle that delegates to the same SDK operation under test is not independent evidence.</p>

</details>
<details data-reference="self-specification-1"><summary>Bootstrap and migration</summary>
<h3>Bootstrap and migration</h3>
<ol>
<li>Pin the current specification sources, working codecs, positive/negative corpus and trusted compiler/checker revision. Inventory each schema-based compatibility gate and map its obligations to identified Morphir rules and independently established expected outcomes.</li>
<li>Compile the reviewed IR/SDK specification sources to model artifacts with the pinned bootstrap toolchain. Validate their structure and selected semantics; execute the selected SDK contracts and codec fixtures. A self-describing IR model does not require the compiler implementation itself to be written in the DSL.</li>
<li>Run the new checks beside the existing gates for the selected scope. Resolve disagreements against the versioned source authority and reviewed fixtures. Historical v1-v3 encoding must preserve the Elm codec authority recorded by this repository; do not copy known schema discrepancies into the new specification.</li>
<li>Switch each fully covered compatibility gate to Morphir-owned validation and remove its JSON Schema execution dependency. Keep remaining gates explicitly unmigrated. Complete this inventory to retire the IR JSON Schema dependency from the compatibility/verification workflow; the narrow first slice alone cannot claim that outcome.</li>
</ol>
<p>For compiler conformance, compile the pinned specification sources with a candidate toolchain, validate the emitted model against the IR/encoding specifications, and compare selected semantics and normalized artifacts against independent fixtures and the bootstrap result. Report these observations separately. Self-validation is evidence within its stated scope, not a proof that the compiler or specification is universally correct. Retain a reproducible bootstrap artifact so a broken candidate does not certify itself as its only judge.</p>
<p>Local evidence includes recursive v4 types in pkgs/morphir-ir/versions/v4/{types,values}.mbt, native SDK integer operations in pkgs/morphir-sdk/basics/basics.mbt, and docs/adr/0001-versioned-ir-models.md. The available finos/morphir checkout describes JSON Schema as the JSON profile's bootstrap definition in docs/user-guides/cli-tools/ir-migrate.md and contains website/static/schemas/morphir-ir-v*. The exact shared compatibility-gate inventory remains a required implementation-planning input; this draft does not claim those gates have already been located or migrated.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="contracts">
<h2>What a check means</h2>
<p>Check admissible inputs, supplied results and preservation rules. No implementation is required to check a candidate result.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Observation</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Satisfied / violated</strong></td><td>An applicable, supported contract was evaluated.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>not_applicable</code></td><td>The operation’s precondition is false.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Invalid input</strong></td><td>An input argument fails its type or refinement.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>inconclusive</code> / <code>unsupported</code></td><td>Required information or execution capability is missing.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Execution error</strong></td><td>Evaluation failed or exhausted its limits.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">A negative test can pass by detecting its expected violation. Keep the test outcome separate from the contract observation.</div>
<details data-reference="contracts-0"><summary>Full contract semantics and execution boundaries</summary>
<p>The first predicate profile supports Bool, arbitrary-precision Int, String, transparent and opaque aliases with refinements, immutable records, tuples, untagged unions of supported member types and algebraic data types with finitely many named constructors, including Result. Parameterized declarations are instantiated at concrete argument types for checking examples. Named types resolve within functional modules and explicit imports. Quantity refinements constrain values without introducing implicit subtype coercions or solver-based assignability. Underlying representations project where the selected IR capability supports them; refinements remain explicit obligations, and public opaque signatures hide the representation.</p>
<p>The IR self-specification path also requires recursive declarations and finite collection validation. Declaration expressiveness, structural validation and executable predicate operations have separate capability inventories: representing a floating-point SDK signature, for example, does not imply that the initial predicate evaluator implements floating-point laws. The self-specification section defines the early coverage boundary.</p>
<p>The declaration language distinguishes transparent aliases, opaque aliases and custom types with constructors. A plain alias names an existing representation. An opaque alias introduces a distinct nominal type whose representation is hidden outside its defining module. A custom type also introduces a distinct identity, including a single-constructor wrapper. Records have structural Morphir record semantics unless enclosed in a nominal type. A refined alias additionally carries a named predicate obligation at its declared uses; erasing its alias name in an IR projection must not erase that obligation. Function types associate to the right. These are proposed semantic choices consistent with Morphir's existing IR, rather than an assertion that every ML-family language makes identical choices.</p>
<p>Supported expressions include literals, parameter/field references, arithmetic addition/subtraction, comparisons, Boolean connectives, conditionals, exhaustive matching and a small versioned set of pure predicates such as string non-emptiness. Bindings and operand types must resolve before execution. Calls to arbitrary user functions, recursion, host effects, clock access and random draws are outside the first predicate profile. There is no general quantifier or temporal-logic solver in this delivery.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Clause</th><th>Bindings and interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Type refinement</td><td><code>self</code> is a value of the underlying type. The predicate determines admissible values.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record invariant</td><td><code>self</code> is the complete record. It can relate multiple fields.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operation precondition</td><td>Named input arguments only. False means the contract does not promise a result for that call.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operation postcondition</td><td>Named inputs and <code>result</code>. All applicable postconditions must hold.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frame condition</td><td>An explicit postcondition states which identities or fields remain unchanged. No implicit preservation of unspecified fields is assumed.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Ordinary business rejection belongs in a Result alternative and its postconditions. A precondition is not automatically a guard that throws, returns an error or prevents an application action. A supported precondition that is false produces <code>not_applicable</code> for that operation contract. Invalid typed inputs produce an invalid-input diagnostic. Missing facts produce <code>inconclusive</code>; unsupported predicate semantics produce <code>unsupported</code>. Evaluator/resource errors are execution errors. None of these is a passing result.</p>
<p>When supplied inputs are well typed and preconditions hold, the checker validates the candidate result's type/refinements and then each postcondition, reporting <code>satisfied</code> or <code>violated</code> with clause identities. Cases record an expected observation so a negative conformance case can pass by correctly detecting a violation. Reports keep the contract observation separate from whether the case matched its expected observation.</p>
<p>Execution is bounded using explicit evaluator limits. Supported arithmetic preserves exact integer meaning; overflow through a machine integer is a defect. A bounded evaluation that exhausts its budget reports an error and cannot establish a guarantee. A supplied candidate can be checked before an operation implementation exists. Computing an output from an arbitrary relational specification is a separate capability and is not promised.</p>
<p>Future profiles can add exact decimals, currency/units, dates and calendars, collection quantification, richer state machines or proof backends. Their semantics and capability claims must be versioned. A single scalar Int example does not establish financial arithmetic or regulatory coverage.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="delivery">
<h2>First delivery</h2>
<p>A portable MoonBit library and CLI, with the IR/SDK slice and rental example using shared checking and evidence contracts.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Proposed command</th><th scope="col">User outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>spec inspect</code></td><td>See requirements, declarations, links and unresolved work.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spec check</code></td><td>Resolve types and evaluate supplied cases.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spec generate</code></td><td>Produce EARS, Gherkin and typed cases with trace manifests.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spec run</code></td><td>Bind an implementation and check actual results.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Command spellings are provisional. Native, JS, Wasm and Wasm-GC share core semantics; host adapters handle I/O.</p>
<details data-reference="delivery-0"><summary>Full command behavior and delivery requirements</summary>
<p>Deliver an offline portable library plus a CLI workflow. The library parses the preview DSL, resolves/types the supported subset, preserves the rich model, checks cases, generates artifacts and emits structured reports. Filesystem, process and publication operations remain in existing host/engine adapters. Native, JS, Wasm and Wasm-GC share core semantics.</p>
<p>Proposed command group: <code>morphir spec inspect</code>, <code>morphir spec check</code>, <code>morphir spec generate</code> and <code>morphir spec run</code>. Command spellings can be adjusted during planning to fit the CLI inventory. Preserve the existing meanings of verify and conform; their current generated-code/evaluator comparisons must not silently become requirement-satisfaction claims.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inspect.</strong> Open prose-only or partially formalized source/checkpoints; show requirements, constructs, links and execution gaps.</li>
<li><strong>Check.</strong> Resolve and typecheck declarations, then check supplied candidate states/results. An explicit selected case set is the execution scope.</li>
<li><strong>Generate.</strong> Emit event-style EARS, Gherkin and typed contract-test cases with trace manifests into an owned output directory.</li>
<li><strong>Run.</strong> Bind an operation by FQName to a caller-supplied current IR model or the explicitly mapped native SDK adapter. Check returned values against the retained contracts. Qualify the selected SDK operations, canonical rental request model and a separately authored partial-return implementation. The IR self-specification workflow also checks supplied model/wire instances against their selected profile.</li>
</ol>
<p>All commands have text, JSON and JSON Lines output. Reports include selection, per-clause observations and coverage gaps. Any required selected case that is violated, invalid, unsupported, inconclusive or encounters an execution error fails the run, except negative cases whose explicit expectation is that observation. An empty selection is reported and cannot establish a passing coverage claim. Malformed invocation/configuration uses exit 2, unsuccessful required checks use exit 1, cancellation uses 130, and successfully completed requested operations use 0. Inspection of a valid but incomplete model can succeed while reporting gaps.</p>
<p>The first delivery includes the IR/SDK dogfooding slice, documented fixed fixtures, a thin shared Gherkin adapter and compatibility run upstream. Draft 0.11 proposes the text/template and Smithy representation additions listed in the <a href="#smithy">coverage gate</a>; full protocol execution remains separate. The subsequent early coverage milestone retires the remaining IR schema gates. It does not require a full Workbench authoring UI, public release/default switch, remote provider install, general Smithy/WIT import/export or new registry packaging. A later Workbench view uses the same inspect/check APIs.</p>
<p>Suggested code boundaries, subject to planning: a new portable conceptual-model package for graph/AST/profile logic; a separate engine frontend/operation adapter; CLI commands in apps/morphir; fixtures in the existing shared testing layout upstream. Reuse naming, current IR, rich data codecs, SDK exact integers, evaluator limits and typed execution results. The implementation plan chooses exact files and public API names after this design is accepted.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="refinement">
<h2>From intent to evidence</h2>
<p>Intent → requirements → concepts and contracts → implementations → evidence. This is a reading order; the stored links form a graph.</p>

<h3>Unfinished knowledge and unfinished source are different</h3>
<p><strong>Maintainer clarification:</strong> preserve the distinction between incremental domain elaboration and incomplete or invalid compiler/editor input. A valid concept can have an agreed meaning while its data representation remains undecided. An editor can also contain a broken reference to a fully understood concept.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Example</th><th>Proposed representation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Domain elaboration</td><td>Borrower is defined, but its identifying information is still under discussion.</td><td>Concept identity, known facts and explicit open questions or missing aspects.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Definition work in progress</td><td>A function body has been started but is unfinished.</td><td>Authored partial definition with its own completeness state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compiler/editor recovery</td><td>A reference was broken by a rename, or an expression is temporarily invalid.</td><td>Recovery information, diagnostics and source snapshot, with partial IR where supported.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review lifecycle</td><td>A complete rule awaits business approval.</td><td>Review status, reviewer and applicable revision.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation availability</td><td>A complete function signature and contract have no implementation.</td><td>A specification with no implementation binding.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<details data-reference="refinement-incompleteness"><summary>Traits, existing IR nodes and lowering boundaries</summary>
<p><strong>Assistant proposal:</strong> use typed traits to attach domain elaboration information, including the unresolved aspect, question, rationale and source. Exact trait names and syntax remain open. The earlier conversational <code>@incomplete type Borrower</code> sketch is not a selected grammar or a decision to lower conceptual gaps to IR holes. A dictionary concept can exist before any corresponding type declaration.</p>
<p>The existing v4 IR has IncompleteTypeDefinition, IncompleteBody, Hole and Draft forms. The parent draft describes holes as broken references and Draft as author-marked work in progress; current MoonBit Draft carries no notes payload. These support best-effort compilation and partial definitions. They are not an automatic encoding of unresolved business knowledge or review lifecycle. A future lowering may reuse a form only when its semantics match, while retaining the reason and origin in the rich model.</p>
<p>Parser recovery and semantic holes also need separate treatment: the current HoleReason alternatives do not describe every possible syntax error. An LSP may retain a recoverable syntax tree and diagnostics before it can produce any typed IR. Incremental compilation also applies to valid source and does not itself imply incompleteness.</p>
<p>A bodyless fn can be a complete specification. Opaque means a representation is hidden, not unknown. A declared type variable is a polymorphic parameter, not a missing type. A concept used where a type is required needs an explicit representation mapping; the checker must report the missing mapping rather than invent Any, a generic parameter or an opaque representation.</p>
<p>Track these concerns independently. A single declaration can have an open domain question and a broken editor reference. Preserve both facts, and report which dependency prevents a requested check. No trait suppresses a type error or turns an unresolved predicate into satisfied evidence.</p>
</details>

<details data-reference="refinement-0"><summary>Full refinement and requirement rules</summary>
<p>Intent → requirements → concepts and contracts → implementations → evidence is a useful reading order. The stored relationships form a graph: one requirement may constrain several operations, and several requirements may constrain the same type. Evidence can reveal a contradiction and prompt an earlier requirement to change.</p>
<p>A prose-only model is valid. Missing types, unresolved links and informal rules appear as gaps. Structural errors such as duplicate identities are invalid; an explicitly unresolved reference remains representable and cannot be executed as though resolved. The reader distinguishes a deliberately declared unresolved target from a misspelled reference to a supposedly known target.</p>
<p>Track independent facts rather than one maturity ladder: source captured, links resolved, expressions well typed, supported for evaluation, implementation bound, evidence available. Human acceptance of an interpretation is another independent fact. A model can contain implemented and conceptual modules together.</p>
<p>Existing <code>morphir kb intent</code> already stores prose with a lifecycle. Preserve and reference its identifiers and document revisions. The initial preview accepts a caller-supplied source reference and retained excerpt; it does not port the KB lifecycle. Likewise, OKF bundles and LLM-wiki documents are sources that can be linked without converting their whole format into the DSL. Automatic source discovery and synchronization are later adapters.</p>
<p>Requirement kinds use named vocabulary entries, initially functional, data, performance, operational and regulatory. A namespace allows additional kinds without changing the parser. EARS pattern categories are separate. A performance requirement can be structurally precise while awaiting measurements in its stated environment.</p>
</details>
</section>


<section id="dictionary">
<h2>Data dictionaries and concept mappings</h2>
<p><strong>Maintainer requirement:</strong> Morphir must create and consume data dictionaries so model declarations can connect to known concepts. Dictionaries are versioned model content, available before implementations or concrete data representations exist.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Dictionary content</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Concept</td><td>Stable identity, preferred name, aliases, definition, context and sources. For example, the quantity still owed on a loan.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data element</td><td>A named representation of a concept with its type, units, format and constraints where known. Several elements may represent the same concept differently.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mapping</td><td>An explicit relationship from a declaration to a concept, or between concepts in different dictionaries, with scope, rationale, provenance and review state.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>A lending model could first define the concept OutstandingQuantity, then link a typed field to it. Another system's field may express the same concept in different units or under different rules. Linking their meanings does not establish type equality or provide a conversion.</p>
<details data-reference="dictionary-profile"><summary>Proposed first delivery and preservation rules</summary>
<p><strong>Assistant proposal:</strong> first support authoring a dictionary in the rich model, publishing it as a versioned model artifact, and consuming an explicitly supplied dictionary dependency. Generate a readable dictionary view from that content and retain authored definitions on round-trip. Exact DSL declaration and trait syntax remain open. Evaluate Semantic Web mappings and exchange profiles while designing the model; adapter delivery and reasoning support can be staged without excluding their requirements from the architecture.</p>
<ul>
<li>Give dictionary entries stable IDs independent of labels, file locations and local import aliases. References identify the dictionary and entry, with the revision or digest recorded in the dependency context. Homonyms in different contexts remain distinct; an alias alone never establishes equivalence.</li>
<li>Keep conceptual meaning separate from data representation. Types, record fields, function parameters/results, requirements and contract terms can link to dictionary entries. A concept is not automatically an IR type. Dictionary-only models are valid; generated views must identify missing definitions instead of inventing them.</li>
<li>Consume dependencies through the same explicit model/build context as imports. Do not fetch an external URL during type checking. Preserve intentionally unresolved links for inspection; malformed or unexpectedly missing declared dependencies produce diagnostics. Any dependent analysis reports the resolution gap.</li>
<li>Keep declaration-to-concept links, such as represents, separate from concept-to-concept mappings. Proposed mapping kinds include exact, close, broader, narrower and related, with explicit direction. Record source and target revisions, scope, rationale, author and review status. Suggested and accepted mappings remain distinguishable; AI suggestions never become accepted mappings automatically.</li>
<li>Use the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/#mapping">SKOS mapping vocabulary</a> as interoperability prior art. Exact and close mappings have different semantics, and close mappings must not be chained into an inferred equivalence. Specify RDF exchange and OWL reasoning capabilities independently. No semantic mapping grants assignability, unit conversion, contract satisfaction or proof without a separately defined and checked rule.</li>
<li>Preserve unknown descriptive dictionary properties in rich round-trips. Refuse or report unsupported required semantics and lossy exports. Dictionary revision changes make dependent mapping reviews and analysis evidence candidates for revalidation; retain the historical claims and observations.</li>
</ul>
<p>This supports the intent funnel: requirement prose can cite a dictionary concept, a data element can represent it, a contract can constrain that element, and generated scenarios can retain those links. The dictionary helps bind vocabulary; executable meaning still requires typed declarations and explicit predicates.</p>
</details>
<p><strong>Maintainer clarification:</strong> retain RDF, N-Quads, OWL, Turtle, TriG and JSON-LD as serious architectural and interoperability candidates. Their roles and delivery profiles need explicit evaluation; none is dismissed by the bounded first delivery.</p>
<details data-reference="dictionary-semantic-web"><summary>RDF, exchange formats and ontology semantics</summary>
<table><thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Role to evaluate for Morphir</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>RDF</td><td>A graph and dataset model for identified concepts, relationships and linked facts. Evaluate mappings to Morphir linked metadata and whether RDF is an exchange representation, an internal representation, or both.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Turtle</td><td>Readable RDF graph interchange for vocabularies, dictionary entries and relationships.</td></tr>
<tr><td>TriG and N-Quads</td><td>RDF dataset interchange preserving default and named graphs. TriG offers readable graph grouping; N-Quads offers line-oriented exchange.</td></tr>
<tr><td>JSON-LD</td><td>Linked-data integration with JSON tooling through explicit contexts, identifiers and graph representations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OWL</td><td>Formal ontology axioms and reasoning, including classification and consistency checks under a declared semantics and supported profile.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Assistant proposal:</strong> define a versioned mapping for concept IDs, typed traits, dictionary entries and claims. Preserve IRIs, literal datatypes, language tags, blank-node relationships and graph membership. Named graphs can organize assertions, but graph names alone do not establish provenance or trust; represent source, revision and assertion ownership explicitly. Pin JSON-LD contexts and ontology imports through dependency resolution so checking does not require uncontrolled network access.</p>
<p>Distinguish syntax support, semantic preservation, vocabulary interpretation, reasoning and executable validation in the capability inventory. OWL uses open-world reasoning: an absent fact need not be false. Its classes, restrictions and unions must not silently become Morphir types, mandatory record fields or runtime unions. A translation needs an explicit rule and stated assumptions. Keep asserted and inferred facts distinguishable, with the inference profile and input revisions recorded. Whether to use an embedded reasoner or an external adapter remains open.</p>
<p>Qualify each selected profile with independent fixtures for named-graph preservation, blank-node scope, literal identity, context/import resolution, and unsupported axioms. Check semantic round-trips up to graph/dataset isomorphism where applicable; byte identity and original formatting are separate source-preservation requirements. Exporting a dataset to graph-only Turtle must expose or refuse graph-boundary loss. Syntax parsing alone does not establish ontology or contract conformance.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="generation">
<h2>What we can generate</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Output</th><th scope="col">First scope</th><th scope="col">What makes it usable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EARS</td><td>Structured event-style clauses.</td><td>Preserve requirement IDs and source interpretation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gherkin</td><td>Readable scenarios and case data.</td><td>Use the existing shared runner and explicit bindings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typed tests</td><td>Authored cases and a finite quantity-boundary recipe.</td><td>Run the checker or a bound implementation; retain expected outcomes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence reports</td><td>Results of actual checks.</td><td>Name revisions, selection, coverage and gaps.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">Generating a test is not passing it. The first generator does not synthesize correct outputs for arbitrary predicates.</div>
<details data-reference="generation-0"><summary>Full generation contracts and limitations</summary>
<table><thead><tr><th>Available information</th><th>Preview output</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Structured event-style requirement</td><td>Deterministically rendered EARS text with requirement ID.</td><td>Free prose is retained without invented formal meaning.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typed examples, operation/contract links and expected observations</td><td>Gherkin scenario outlines and examples, with identity tags and a trace manifest.</td><td>Steps have specified bindings; otherwise output is identified as unbound.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inputs and candidate results for a contract</td><td>Runnable contract test definitions for the local specification checker.</td><td>They check candidate transitions; they do not invoke an absent operation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resolved implementation binding</td><td>The same cases invoke that implementation and check its results.</td><td>Actual execution produces a separate evidence report.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The first generator renders authored, independently reviewed cases. It also includes one explicit finite quantity-boundary recipe around zero and the outstanding balance; the recipe lists generated inputs and invokes the contract checker as an oracle where a candidate is supplied. It does not infer a correct output for arbitrary predicates. Random property generation, shrinking, solver-based input synthesis and AI generation of proposed requirements are later capabilities.</p>
<p>Use the existing shared Gherkin foundation, morphir-gherkin/morphir-bdd and its testing contracts. The preview emits standard Gherkin plus typed case data and a small explicit binding adapter for this contract-check vocabulary. Parsing and execution compatibility is qualified with a pinned shared runner in finos/morphir. The portable MoonBit core can check its typed cases directly, without embedding a second Gherkin parser/runner.</p>
<p>Generator outputs carry a manifest of source model revision, entity IDs, generator profile/version and artifact digests. Repeating generation with the same semantic inputs and configuration is deterministic. Write to an owned output directory; fail on modified generated files unless replacement is explicitly requested. Authored scenarios live separately and can reference the generated model. Round-tripping arbitrary edited prose or Gherkin into exact original contracts is not assumed.</p>
<p>A generator cannot label a test runnable without an available checker/binding. Unsupported generation targets return a diagnostic. Generated test definitions and successful generation do not add passing execution evidence. Generating both implementation and tests from the same rule is useful but does not replace independent examples or review.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="evidence">
<h2>Claims need evidence</h2>
<p>Connect prose to constructs through typed metadata, and connect claims to scoped evidence.</p><pre><code class="language-moonbit">intent → requirement → contract → scenario
scenario → execution → evidence</code></pre><p>Each observation names the relevant model, requirement and implementation revisions. Changed revisions make prior evidence stale for the new context.</p>
<details data-reference="evidence-0"><summary>Full metadata, claim and evidence rules</summary>
<p>The built-in vocabulary declares relationship signatures such as requirement <code>derivedFrom</code> source fragment, requirement <code>refines</code> intent, contract <code>formalizes</code> requirement, operation <code>governedBy</code> contract, scenario <code>exercises</code> contract, and evidence <code>supports</code>/<code>contradicts</code> claim. Validate subject/object kinds. Extend the vocabulary through named schemas; descriptive unknown predicates remain opaque and retained.</p>
<p>Every authored entity has a stable logical ID independent of file position. Model revisions identify snapshots. A source reference records document identity, revision/content identity, fragment anchor and retained wording where available. External references are never implicitly fetched during checking; unresolved or unpinned references are reported as such. An explicitly requested source refresh creates new source facts and triggers reassessment.</p>
<p>A claim records subject, proposition, author/provenance, relevant model/requirement revisions, scope and links to evidence. Human acceptance of a proposed formalization is a review assertion. Runtime provider capability claims retain their existing separate meaning. A graph relation named implements is an assertion; it does not establish satisfaction.</p>
<p>Run evidence includes the specification and implementation identities, input and observed output, evaluated clauses, evaluator/profile version, relevant environment/configuration and limits. Generated cases additionally retain generator version and seed/configuration. Claims can have supporting and contradicting evidence simultaneously. Lack of evidence is unknown, not false; a type check alone is not behavioral evidence.</p>
<p>First-delivery inspection reports unresolved links, requirements without contracts or examples, unsupported clauses, missing implementation bindings and stale evidence. General contradiction detection and proof of equivalence to prose are not preview features. Independent domain review and manually established expected results remain necessary evidence about the formalization itself.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="rentals">
<h2>One worked example</h2>
<p>The proposed partial-return example gives the checker an input and a candidate result.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Outstanding / returned</th><th scope="col">Candidate</th><th scope="col">Expected observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Balance 60; same loan and asset.</td><td>Satisfied</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Balance 70.</td><td>Violated balance rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Balance 60; different asset.</td><td>Violated preservation rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 101</td><td>OverReturn.</td><td>Satisfied rejection rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / −1</td><td>Any result.</td><td>Invalid refined input</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The existing canonical rental request fixture keeps its historical behavior. This return workflow is a separate educational extension.</p>
<details data-reference="rentals-0"><summary>Full rental cases, generation recipe and Gherkin binding</summary>
<p>The canonical fixture already in this repository defines <code>request(availability, requestedQuantity)</code>: return <code>Ok(requestedQuantity)</code> when requestedQuantity is at most availability; otherwise return <code>Err("Insufficient availability")</code>. Its inputs are Int, with no nonnegative precondition. Preserve this behavior exactly in a compatibility example, including a negative-input case. Adding a nonnegative domain policy is a new requirement, not a correction to that fixture.</p>
<p>The new partial-return example is a separate educational extension. It describes one loan, one asset measured in indivisible units, and a pure operation. A caller has already selected the loan and verified asset identity. No settlement, custody, valuation, fee, event persistence or duplicate-event policy is implied. Those become named requirements in the securities-lending continuation.</p>
<p><code>Quantity</code> is an exact integer at least zero. A Loan has id, asset and outstanding quantity. For <code>acceptReturn(loan, returned)</code>, an amount at most the outstanding balance returns Ok with the outstanding balance reduced by that amount and id/asset preserved. A larger amount returns OverReturn. Zero is accepted as an unchanged loan. Inputs with negative quantities are invalid according to the type refinement. These are assistant-proposed rules for the demonstration.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Outstanding / returned</th><th>Candidate result or implementation expectation</th><th>Contract observation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Ok with 60, same id and asset</td><td>Satisfied</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Ok with 70</td><td>Violated: balance clause</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>Ok with 60 and a different asset</td><td>Violated: frame clause</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 100</td><td>Ok with 0</td><td>Satisfied</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 0</td><td>Ok with 100</td><td>Satisfied</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 101</td><td>OverReturn</td><td>Satisfied: modeled rejection</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 101</td><td>Ok with any balance</td><td>Violated: rejection clause</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / -1</td><td>Any</td><td>Invalid typed input</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 / 40</td><td>No candidate and no implementation binding</td><td>Inconclusive: missing result</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Boundary generation for a concrete balance b emits the unique valid inputs from {0, 1, b-1, b, b+1}, omitting negatives. Invalid-input examples are a separate explicit suite. At b=0 this produces {0, 1}. Exact integers above 2^53 and machine integer range must also appear in independently supplied cases.</p>
<p>The generated EARS sentence for LENDING-RETURN-001 is: "When a return is accepted, the lending system shall reduce the outstanding quantity by the accepted quantity." Separate linked requirements express over-return rejection and identity preservation. Their existence is explicit; the generator does not invent them from the balance sentence.</p>
<details><summary>Generated Gherkin behavior to qualify</summary>
<p>A feature contains a tagged scenario outline that supplies loan data, returned quantity and candidate result, invokes the named contract checker, and compares its observation with the expected observation from the table. A separate bound feature invokes the implementation instead of supplying the candidate. The step adapter preserves exact integer lexemes and reports missing implementation bindings as unresolved. Requirement and clause identities are carried in tags and the trace manifest; fixture values remain in typed case data.</p>
</details>
</details>
</section>

<section id="representation">
<h2>What the model carries</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model content</th><th scope="col">Preservation rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IR + conceptual graph</td><td>Use the rich IRUnit carrier and a versioned profile.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prose + requirements + contracts</td><td>Retain IDs, source links, predicates and revisions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metadata + unknown descriptive data</td><td>Preserve it through rich checkpoints.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Untagged unions</td><td>Map to the proposed core IR Union node; report capability gaps until adopted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bare IR export</td><td>Refuse required-content loss or unsupported projection.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<details data-reference="representation-0"><summary>Full IR, identity and preservation rules</summary>
<p>Retain a single logical Morphir model with independent model revision, IR format and representation/profile versions. For the preview, name the conceptual profile <code>morphir-conceptual/0.1.0-draft.1</code>; this is a proposed identifier, not a released format.</p>
<p>Use an existing <code>IRUnit</code> checkpoint. Its valid <code>unit:&lt;unit-id&gt;</code> metadata owner holds the conceptual document graph as typed Ion data. Identities of requirements and prose fragments are graph identities inside that payload, not invented IR metadata owners. Existing ownership validation remains intact. Other node metadata and unknown Ion properties/annotations are retained.</p>
<p>A prose-only model has an empty <code>Specs</code> distribution as its compatibility carrier. Resolved declarations project into the primary package supplied by the declared build/manifest context and its module specifications when the selected IR capability supports them. Source namespaces qualify model/module names; they do not introduce additional primary packages or select a package by splitting a dotted name. Unresolved constructs remain in the conceptual graph. Union declarations resolve in that graph and project to the proposed IR 4.0.0 Union node once the consumer implements it. An older carrier may preserve the complete typed declaration graph while omitting affected IR declarations and their dependent signatures, but must list those projection gaps; it is not a complete public specification. Bare IR export refuses the unsupported projection. Signature-only operations otherwise project into Specs; implementations supplied later remain separate identified model inputs bound by FQName.</p>
<p>Contract source and resolved typed expression are retained together. Compiled predicate functions live in a derived executable artifact, linked to their source contract and compiler profile; they are not alternative authored authority. The shared contract records how their binders map to function arguments. The preview may validate this graph using built-in profile semantics; optional metadata-provider authentication is not a baseline requirement.</p>
<p>Ion text and binary checkpoints retain equivalent information using the existing data layer. Standard bare IR JSON export must refuse when it would discard required conceptual content or contracts, using the existing loss-refusal behavior. This preview does not add a lossy JSON shortcut or claim native support for draft IR 4.1.0. JSON/JSON Lines command reports remain supported.</p>
<p>A rich consumer may preserve unknown descriptive facts. A consumer asked to check a required semantic contract must either support that profile or return unsupported. No generator may report checked contract fulfillment after silently erasing refinements. Converting a definition into a specification currently initializes annotations to empty; all new paths must preserve the separately retained contract graph and test that transition.</p>
<p>Changing identities requires an explicit mapping. For the preview, evidence matches exact recorded model/source/implementation revisions. A changed revision marks old evidence stale for the new evaluation context; it remains a valid historical observation. Fine-grained preservation proofs across unrelated changes are later work.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="acceptance">
<h2>Review and acceptance</h2>
<p>Use these review lenses; expand the exact acceptance cases below.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Evidence to inspect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Language</td><td>Enclosing scopes, multiple modules per file, imports, aliases, opacity, unions, generics, arbitrary tuple arity and named fn binders.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-specification</td><td>Recursive IR fixtures, encoding rules, native SDK cases and bootstrap comparisons.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Correct outcomes, deliberate mutants, refinement failures and honest unsupported/error reports.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preservation</td><td>Rich round-trips, identity/revision links and refused lossy exports.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Portable targets, installed CLI workflows and shared Gherkin compatibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">Independent expected outcomes remain necessary alongside generated tests.</div>
<details data-reference="acceptance-0"><summary>Exact acceptance criteria and independent checks</summary>
<ol>
<li>Independent fixtures distinguish a valid concept awaiting representation, an unfinished definition, an editor recovery hole, a rule awaiting review, and a complete bodyless fn. Combined cases preserve each concern. No conceptual trait suppresses diagnostics or fabricates types, implementations or passing evidence.</li>
<li>Create, export and consume a versioned dictionary with definitions, aliases, concept/data-element distinctions and declaration links. Round-trips preserve IDs, revisions and provenance. Cases cover homonyms, missing representations, unresolved external links, mapping direction, suggested versus accepted mappings, changed dictionary revisions and unsupported properties. Semantic mappings never silently enable assignment or conversion. Generated dictionary views do not fabricate missing definitions.</li>
<li>A prose-only model round-trips through Ion text/binary with identity, source text and unknown descriptive metadata intact. Its inspection lists unresolved formalization honestly.</li>
<li>Raw multiline literals work in requirement text, metadata and String-valued examples. Independent fixtures cover empty text, interior blank lines, final LF, significant spaces/tabs, indented markers, Unicode, quotes, backslashes, apparent interpolation, embedded markers and comment tokens. LF/CRLF source forms decode identically. Unprefixed lines end the block; malformed holes and invalid adjacent blocks produce diagnostics. Raw lines never interpolate. String values survive IR/model/source round-trips, with exact original spelling retained in source metadata when required.</li>
<li>Typed templates retain declared parameters and source segments before rendering. Fixtures cover mixed raw/interpolated lines, literal delimiters, missing/unknown names, incompatible and optional arguments, empty/multiline inserted values, no recursive expansion, exact Int/Bool formatting, opaque-value rejection, deterministic output and evaluator limits. Imported Smithy placeholders retain their dialect and arguments; unsupported exports report gaps. Generated target documents pass their own syntax validators.</li>
<li>The pinned Smithy inventory covers all four requested families and their dependencies, with no missing feature hidden as generic metadata. Validate and round-trip condition definitions, service/operation bindings, protocol applications and AI prompts. Include misplaced traits, unresolved references, nontrivial condition expressions, foreign template dialects, optional/default fields, precision and tagged-union cases. Reject document use in the inspected CBOR profile. Follow-on parity requires independent JMESPath and protocol wire/event-stream evidence; representation-only coverage cannot claim it.</li>
<li>The pinned IR Type specification compiles and validates its dependency-closed fixture set, including recursive types, attributes, maps/lists and malformed nested data. Alias cycles fail; bounded recursive checking reports exhaustion without passing.</li>
<li>The selected wire profile rejects malformed raw envelopes/tags/payloads before normalization loses information. Reviewed positive/negative fixtures and codec round-trips exercise every replaced schema obligation, with disagreements resolved against the versioned authority.</li>
<li>Selected SDK integer add/subtract/abs bindings satisfy independent cases, including exact large integers. Deliberate SDK and codec mutants fail. Candidate compiler output is checked against the self-specification and compared with pinned bootstrap/independent fixtures.</li>
<li>For a migrated gate, the compatibility workflow succeeds with its JSON Schema validator absent. A coverage manifest names unmigrated gates and unsupported rules. The follow-on early milestone removes the remaining IR schema dependencies only when the full inventory has passed equivalent or stronger checks.</li>
<li>Equivalent nested, dotted and line-level enclosing-scope forms resolve to the same qualified model/module names. Namespace-plus-module and fully qualified module declarations resolve identically under the same explicit build package. Fixtures cover sibling headers, scope restoration, imports, conceptual links, reopening and normalized-name collisions. A namespace change does not select another IR package. Reject unresolved model/build contexts and conflicting model metadata. Rich round-trips preserve scope and artifact identity; a module-only source must not erase model metadata.</li>
<li>A group of named types and operation signatures resolves within modules, with useful diagnostics for duplicate IDs, unresolved declared references, invalid links and ill-typed clauses.</li>
<li>Two or more modules in one file compile like equivalent modules in separate declared source files. Block and successive-header forms produce identical module identities; imports do not leak between them. Fixtures cover forward imports, aliases, selected public names, explicit dependency aliases, conflicting/unknown/private imports, opaque constructors, guarded recursive types and invalid alias cycles. Splitting, combining or reordering source files preserves resolved semantics when each declaration retains its namespace/import environment and the same build context. Importing retains declaration identity/contracts and does not imply source fetching, textual inclusion, re-export or executable implementation.</li>
<li>Bodyless fn declarations project named, ordered inputs and the exact output type. Contracts resolve those binders without a separate argument-name list. Duplicate/reserved binders fail; two inputs remain distinct from one tuple input, and a function-valued output is not flattened into inputs. Declaring a specification alone creates no executable implementation.</li>
<li>Declarations distinguish <code>type alias A = B</code> from <code>type A = B</code> without name-resolution heuristics. Algebraic declarations with and without the optional leading pipe produce equivalent semantic models. Transparent record aliases retain structural identity; opaque records hide fields and opaque variants hide constructors outside their defining module. Aliasing an opaque type preserves its identity and encapsulation.</li>
<li>Parameterized aliases, recursive algebraic types, phantom-parameter opaque types and rank-1 polymorphic signatures parse, resolve and preserve their binders through IR projection. Arity, unbound/duplicate declaration variables and inconsistent concrete substitutions produce diagnostics. Alpha-renaming preserves meaning; generic contract reports identify tested substitutions and do not imply universal proof or supported higher-order execution.</li>
<li>Tuple types, values and patterns work beyond pairs/triples, including generic four- and eight-element fixtures. Projection and codec round-trips preserve exact arity and order; wrong arity or incompatible element types fail. A flat tuple and nested tuples remain distinct. Inspect the implementation for arbitrary tuple-arity caps in addition to these finite examples.</li>
<li>Untagged union cases accept String and Int identifiers, reject Bool, and require narrowing before member-specific operations. Generic substitutions, reordered/duplicate/overlapping alternatives and nested unions preserve membership semantics. Ambiguous wire decoding produces a diagnostic rather than selecting the first member. Opaque members retain their identity.</li>
<li>Union-aware IR projection preserves the first-class draft Union node, its member expressions and attributes. Consumers without that capability report unsupported projection/execution, and rich checkpoints retain the complete conceptual declarations and explicit projection gaps. Union support in the IR/SDK self-specification and compatibility corpus is an early tracked requirement.</li>
<li>Opaque aliases with identical base types remain incompatible with each other and with their base type. Imported modules cannot access their representation constructors. A zero-valued PositiveQuantity fixture is rejected; a positive fixture is accepted. Public signature projection hides the representation, and rich model round-trips preserve nominal identity, source form and refinement obligations.</li>
<li>The partial-return table produces the independently specified observations before an implementation exists, including frame-condition failure and exact large integers.</li>
<li>A separate precondition example demonstrates not_applicable. Missing values, unsupported predicates and exhausted limits never masquerade as satisfied.</li>
<li>Generation produces valid EARS for the supported pattern, shared-runner-accepted Gherkin and runnable contract cases with source/revision links. Repeating generation yields identical artifacts.</li>
<li>The same contract cases run against an explicitly bound implementation. Deliberately incorrect balance, wrong error variant and changed-asset implementations are detected.</li>
<li>The canonical rental request fixture retains its original semantics, including negative values. New domain refinements do not rewrite it.</li>
<li>Changes to requirement/model/implementation revisions mark previous evidence stale for the new context. Historical observations remain inspectable.</li>
<li>Unknown descriptive metadata survives; unknown required semantic clauses refuse execution. Bare IR export refuses required-content loss. Specs/implementation transitions preserve contract links.</li>
<li>Portable library checks pass on supported MoonBit targets; installed native and npm CLI workflows exercise the public commands. Shared Gherkin acceptance runs with a pinned upstream runner/adapter.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use manually established expected observations and existing canonical fixtures in addition to generated cases. Mutation examples test whether the checker notices a wrong implementation. Shared MCK integration adds cases to the existing runner/corpus; it does not redefine every model test as a compatibility test. Run repository-required lint, format and validation gates before any code push.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section id="terms">
<h2>Vocabulary</h2>
<p><strong>Specification</strong> already means public IR types and signatures. An <strong>executable specification</strong> adds evaluable rules or scenarios linked to the intent they formalize.</p>
<details data-reference="terms-0"><summary>Full glossary and module boundaries</summary>
<p>The existing glossary uses <em>Specification</em> for public module/package types and signatures. The new term <em>executable specification</em> is separate and does not redefine that IR construct.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning in this design</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Intent</td><td>An identified statement of a desired outcome, retaining its author, sources, rationale and unresolved questions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Requirement</td><td>An identified obligation or property derived from intent or an external source. Categories are extensible and independent of its notation or executability.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PRD</td><td>An authored document that organizes requirements and explains scope and rationale. It is one possible view of the model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ConceptualModel</td><td>A Morphir model profile retaining concepts, requirements and their relationships before all representations or implementations are known.</td></tr>
<tr><td>IR package</td><td>The ownership and dependency identity supplied by the compilation context, distinct from the source naming-scope keyword.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enclosing naming scope</td><td>A hierarchical prefix for source model/module names. Namespace and package are alternative keyword candidates; examples use namespace provisionally.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Construct</td><td>An identified declaration such as a domain concept, type or operation. Its declared kind determines the semantics of its properties.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data dictionary</td><td>An identified, versioned collection of concept and data-element definitions, their relationships and sources.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concept mapping</td><td>A scoped assertion relating concepts, with provenance and review status; it does not establish technical type equivalence.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contract</td><td>Identified typed predicates specifying admissible values, states or input/output relationships.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Executable specification</td><td>Requirements expressed as evaluable rules or scenarios within a Morphir model, linked to the intent they formalize.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scenario</td><td>Supplied inputs or states, an action or proposed result, and an expected observation. Its source and expected result remain identifiable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim</td><td>An assertion about a specified subject and proposition within an explicit revision and scope.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence</td><td>A recorded observation, analysis result or review associated with a claim. A test definition is not execution evidence.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Assistant vocabulary proposal:</strong> call an extensible, typed property a <em>trait</em>, its schema a trait definition, and its attachment a trait application. Annotation describes an authoring notation such as @. Linked metadata remains the underlying mechanism; existing IR attributes and graph facts retain their distinct representation roles. A descriptive tag need not carry semantic obligations. Trait vocabulary and exact spelling remain under review.</p>
<p>A module groups domain declarations in Morphir's functional sense. An enclosing namespace supplies naming scope for models and modules; package is an alternative source spelling under discussion. A WIT world describes required/provided interfaces; a Smithy service describes an API grouping. Their mappings need explicit rules rather than treating them as synonyms.</p>
</details>
</section>

