<main>
<h1>MoonBit String source semantics</h1><p class="meta">morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.1 · revision 0.1 · 17 October 2026 · proposed, awaiting semantic review</p><nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents"><ul><li><a href="#outcome">A runnable MoonBit String example first</a></li><li><a href="#evidence-summary">Compiler evidence that changes the design</a></li><li><a href="#profiles">Additive profiles and dependency boundaries</a></li><li><a href="#source-contract">Concrete grammar and type rules</a></li><li><a href="#lowering">Executable IR semantics, rather than descriptive width or encoding facts</a></li><li><a href="#boundaries">A source String is well-formed UTF-16</a></li><li><a href="#operations">Ordering and four pinned source predicate methods</a></li><li><a href="#indexing">Length and owned slices wait only for N1</a></li><li><a href="#metadata">Metadata ownership and format preservation</a></li><li><a href="#resources">Proposed limits and failure policy</a></li><li><a href="#delivery">Implementation contracts and shared ownership</a></li><li><a href="#evidence">Reproducible evidence and its limits</a></li><li><a href="#review">Decisions to review before implementation</a></li></ul></nav><section id="outcome"><h2>A runnable MoonBit String example first</h2><p>Compile explicitly typed MoonBit String/Bool libraries through the existing engine and CLI, evaluate their typed IR, generate callable MoonBit, and expose a working greeting sample in the workbench. String basics consumes the shared typed-library milestone, independently of numeric N2-N6. Keep the original Boolean APIs and default profiles compatible.</p><p class="note">Draft semantic specification revision 0.1 for maintainer review. Scheduling is already approved. These probes qualify raw source/core and hand-authored typed IR, not a completed String source frontend or installed profile.</p><pre><code class="language-moonbit">fn prefix() -&gt; String { &quot;Hello, &quot; }
pub fn greet(name : String) -&gt; String {
  let greeting : String = prefix()
  greeting + name
}
pub fn welcome(name : String, enabled : Bool) -&gt; String {
  if enabled { greet(name) } else { &quot;&quot; }
}</code></pre><p>The workbench sample is MoonBit source in the additive String basics profile. Evaluate <code>greet("Morphir")</code> as <code>"Hello, Morphir"</code>; evaluate both branches of <code>welcome</code>; inspect the generated library and invoke its public entries. Private <code>prefix</code> remains inaccessible to external consumers.</p></section><section id="evidence-summary"><h2>Compiler evidence that changes the design</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Pinned MoonBit source/core</th><th>Morphir SDK Text</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equality</td><td>&quot;é&quot; differs from &quot;e + combining acute&quot;</td><td>Exact UTF-16 units also differ</td><td>No Unicode normalization or locale coercion.</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering</td><td>&quot;b&quot; &lt; &quot;aa&quot; is true, shortlex</td><td>Lexical comparison says false</td><td>Length-first comparison, then UTF-16 lexical tie-break.</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>&quot;a😀b&quot; has 4 UTF-16 units</td><td>SDK length also yields 4</td><td>Expose Int32 result only with the indexed slice after N1.</td></tr><tr><td>Split surrogate</td><td>&quot;a😀b&quot;[1:2].to_owned() is empty</td><td>SDK slice retains the high surrogate 55357</td><td>Use source-specific slice lowering, never a direct SDK slice substitution.</td></tr><tr><td>Negative slice start</td><td>[−1:3] clamps start to zero, returns &quot;a😀&quot;</td><td>SDK negative offset is relative to the end, returns empty</td><td>Clamp source indexes to [0, length].</td></tr><tr><td>Uppercase</td><td>&quot;Straße&quot; becomes &quot;STRAßE&quot;</td><td>SDK becomes &quot;STRASSE&quot;</td><td>Defer case conversion; source ASCII mapping differs from SDK Unicode mapping.</td></tr><tr><td>Lowercase</td><td>&quot;ΟΣ&quot; stays &quot;ΟΣ&quot;</td><td>SDK becomes &quot;ος&quot;</td><td>Defer case conversion.</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid source literal</td><td>Compiler refuses \u{d800}, diagnostic 4064</td><td>SDK Text can store isolated halves</td><td>Source String must remain well-formed UTF-16.</td></tr><tr><td>Host conversion</td><td>Source String is well formed</td><td>Text::to_string raises UnrepresentableText for a lone surrogate</td><td>Reject source-boundary invalid input; preserve general Morphir Text behavior.</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section id="profiles"><h2>Additive profiles and dependency boundaries</h2><p>Keep source language <code>moonbit</code>. The following profile IDs are proposed versioned contracts, not current registrations. Separate immutable profiles let each complete slice be offered without silently changing a previously qualified subset. The combined profile is offered only after the final join.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Proposed profile</th><th>Admitted source subset</th><th>Prerequisites</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>moonbit-model-string-library-v1</td><td>String/Bool basics only</td><td>Reviewed design + shared typed foundation morphir-ukvn.1.10.12.3</td></tr><tr><td>moonbit-model-string-ordered-library-v1</td><td>Basics + &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;= on String</td><td>String basics; no source numeric types required</td></tr><tr><td>moonbit-model-string-indexed-library-v1</td><td>Basics + qualified N1 Int32 forms + length and owned slices</td><td>String basics + full N1 Int32 qualification</td></tr><tr><td>moonbit-model-string-sdk-library-v1</td><td>Basics + four pinned source predicate methods</td><td>String basics + shared resolver morphir-ukvn.1.10.12.4</td></tr><tr><td>moonbit-model-string-qualified-library-v1</td><td>Union of the four individually qualified String subsets</td><td>All String slices + installed String qualification</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Existing Boolean profiles/default remain as they are. The new source descriptors list actual grammar, concrete signatures, runtime-extension requirements and target/mode qualification; they do not claim every String SDK binding or grant a host capability. Numeric N6 and String installed qualification remain independent.</p></section><section id="source-contract"><h2>Concrete grammar and type rules</h2><ul><li>Basics accepts one-file acyclic libraries, explicitly typed ordinary functions with positional named parameters and explicit String/Bool results, public or unexported visibility, ordinary cooked String literals, Boolean literals, parameter/local references, grouping, same-type constraints, immutable single-name lets, direct fully applied same-file calls, and if/else with Bool conditions and identical branch types.</li><li>String + String returns String. String == String and String != String return Bool. Existing Boolean operations keep their semantics. No String/Bool coercion, interpolation, multiline strings, Char, StringView parameters/results, generics, closures, partial applications, imports, mutation, effects, early returns, loops or recursion is admitted by basics.</li><li>Collect concrete signatures before resolving bodies. Resolve binders and calls once, type every expression, require exact arguments/results and reject unresolved or mismatched uses. Preserve readable names, lexical shadowing, source evaluation order, visibility and existing cancellation/observation phases.</li><li>Validate lexer errors before accepting parser output. The pinned parser accepts the proposed String syntax, while the current Boolean frontend rejects it with moonbit_frontend.unsupported_type. Parser acceptance alone does not count as frontend admission.</li><li>Retain original source literal spelling and its validated source location separately from cooked text. Use the lexer coordinate adapter and original token/source record, never reconstruct the authored token from the decoded AST.</li></ul></section><section id="lowering"><h2>Executable IR semantics, rather than descriptive width or encoding facts</h2><p>Reuse the checked mixed-type library pipeline from the shared foundation. Source String uses classic <code>morphir/SDK:string#string</code> as its semantic IR value carrier and <code>sdk.Text</code> in the generated ABI. Source Bool uses the existing Boolean carrier. Every expression, binding, call and type use carries its actual type and its own source origin.</p><p>Keep String validity and resource behavior executable. Introduce a separately declared, versioned source-runtime extension <code>morphir-moonbit-source-string-v1</code> with the proposed identities below. These are internal compiler lowering targets, not source-importable SDK names. Their implementation must be registered consistently in the explicit extension specification/runtime inventory, evaluator, generator and invocation qualification. The classic 248-binding SDK inventory and its semantic pin remain unchanged.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Proposed internal identity</th><th>Contract</th><th>Needed by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>morphir/moonbit-source:string#require-valid</td><td>Text plus configured code-unit bound → same Text; reject malformed UTF-16 or excess length</td><td>All source String public entry boundaries</td></tr><tr><td>morphir/moonbit-source:string#append-bounded</td><td>Two valid Text operands plus bound → concatenated valid Text; check output size before allocation</td><td>String basics + operator</td></tr><tr><td>morphir/moonbit-source:string#slice-owned-bounded</td><td>Valid Text and qualified Int32 bounds plus bound → source-clamped, pair-trimmed owned Text</td><td>Indexed profile only</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not duplicate classic Text append or storage. The source helpers add the missing validation, size preflight and slice policy around existing implementations. Typed IR references declare the required extension explicitly. Missing or wrong-signature runtime bindings refuse before publication; a similarly named package cannot register arbitrary native code.</p><p>Evaluate receiver, operands and arguments once in source order, using explicit temporaries before curried IR applications. Emit public entry checks after all source arguments have been evaluated and before the body. Internal calls retain the same source evaluation order. Direct generated consumers also execute these guards, so safety does not depend solely on an installed host recognizing metadata.</p></section><section id="boundaries"><h2>A source String is well-formed UTF-16</h2><p>The source String invariant is stricter than general Morphir Text. Typed invocation may transport arbitrary Text code units, but a source String entry rejects isolated high or low surrogates, reversed pairs and truncated pairs before the body. A generated public export enforces the same rule. Well-formed input preserves its exact code units, including supplementary and combining sequences; it is never normalized, repaired or replaced.</p><p>Use existing <code>SdkError.UnrepresentableText</code> for direct consumer conversion/validity failures and the evaluator's existing <code>sdk.unrepresentable_text</code> classification. Use <code>SdkError.OutOfRange</code> and the existing SDK range classification for declared source-size policy refusals. Keep frontend typing/syntax failures contextual using the shared frontend diagnostic contract. New boundary/extension diagnostic spellings must be registered and tested, not inferred from metadata.</p><p>The first backend probes intentionally retain and compare a lone surrogate as SDK Text and verify host String conversion refusal. This remains legal in general Morphir Text programs. It does not become legal source String input because backend Text operations can retain it.</p><details><summary>Proposed invocation sequence</summary><p>This is the planned source entry contract, not a claim that the current workbench implements it. <a href="../strings-source-design/diagrams/invocation.html">Open the explorable sequence</a>.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 920 774" data-sequence-column-fit="spread" data-reader-fit="width-first" role="img" lang="en" aria-labelledby="archify-diagram-title archify-diagram-description" data-anima