      </svg></details></section><section id="operations"><h2>Ordering and four pinned source predicate methods</h2><p>String ordering is shortlex over UTF-16 units. Evaluate both operands once; compare lengths first; if equal, use the SDK UTF-16 lexical ordering. The design probe demonstrates this explicit typed-IR composition through Scheme and generated consumers, including equal-length supplementary versus BMP text. Do not lower source ordering directly to classic Basics Text comparison. The ordered profile initially exposes only Boolean comparison operators; Int32-returning compare methods remain outside it.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Pinned source method</th><th>Canonical implementation target</th><th>Argument policy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>s.is_empty()</td><td>morphir/SDK:string#is-empty</td><td>Valid String receiver, no arguments</td></tr><tr><td>s.contains(needle)</td><td>morphir/SDK:string#contains</td><td>Receiver and String needle; swap into SDK needle-first argument order</td></tr><tr><td>s.has_prefix(prefix)</td><td>morphir/SDK:string#starts-with</td><td>Receiver and String prefix; explicit pinned String-to-view coercion</td></tr><tr><td>s.has_suffix(suffix)</td><td>morphir/SDK:string#ends-with</td><td>Receiver and String suffix; explicit pinned String-to-view coercion</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The original compiler accepts String variables as predicate arguments through its implicit StringView coercion. Freeze that exact builtin mapping in the shared resolver. No first-class or escaping StringView value is admitted. Unknown methods, wrong signatures, shadowed bindings or non-admitted coercions refuse; the name alone cannot select a package or runtime function.</p><p>Case conversion, trimming, replacement, reverse, regex, locale behavior, interpolation, iteration, callback-based methods, List/Maybe-returning methods and numeric parsing/formatting are deferred. In particular, current MoonBit ASCII-only to_upper/to_lower must not be mapped to SDK Unicode case conversion. Future additions need their own contract and per-function evidence.</p></section><section id="indexing"><h2>Length and owned slices wait only for N1</h2><p>The indexed profile uses qualified N1 Int32 source boundaries and its actual SDK wrapper ABI. <code>s.length()</code> returns the UTF-16 unit count as Int32 after exact checked conversion. Do not equate it with grapheme or Unicode scalar count.</p><p>Initially admit only an owned slice expression <code>s[start:end].to_owned()</code>, with both bounds supplied as Int32 expressions and the receiver a String. The view is an internal intermediate and cannot escape into variables, parameters or results. Fully evaluate receiver, start and end once before lowering.</p><pre><code class="language-text">n = UTF-16 unit length
start = clamp(start, 0, n)
end = clamp(end, 0, n)
if start is inside a surrogate pair: start += 1
if end is inside a surrogate pair: end -= 1
if start &gt;= end: return empty String
otherwise return the exact units in [start, end)</code></pre><p>Negative indexes clamp to zero; they are not relative offsets. Split supplementary characters disappear from that slice rather than becoming isolated halves. Combining marks may be sliced independently because the contract is code-unit based, not grapheme based. Reject exact_view/get_view/legacy view and borrowed StringView APIs until separately specified. No unchecked slicing or panic-based partial match is a lowering strategy.</p></section><section id="metadata"><h2>Metadata ownership and format preservation</h2><p>Propose a schema-validated <code>moonbit-source-string-v1</code> representation payload on each String parameter/output type use and original expression. Record language, authored/canonical type spelling, UTF-16 encoding, well-formedness invariant, compiler/profile identity and owning source origin. Record literal token spelling/cooked units on the original literal, and conversion/synthetic-operation policy on the implementing expression with an origin link.</p><p>Use existing TypeAttributes.extensions, ValueAttributes.extensions and addressed IRUnit.metadata. Share the ownership and schema dispatch conventions with the numeric foundation, without overwriting numeric or unknown neighboring facts. Final extension keys must be declared with the shared schema owner; this document's contract ID is a proposal, not a registered Morphir predicate.</p><p>Literal text and authored source facts describe the program. Required validation and ordering/slicing behavior reside in executable IR and declared runtime-extension dependencies. Wire metadata cannot register a runtime binding, authenticate a provider, authorize an optimization or grant host access.</p><p>Ion text/binary remains the preferred checkpoint representation. Preserve unknown metadata and source/target naming records on replay. Morphir JSON remains supported where the current checked projection retains facts; preserve data.lossy_conversion refusals rather than discarding engine metadata/provenance. No new IR revision or native property-graph codec support is claimed.</p></section><section id="resources"><h2>Proposed limits and failure policy</h2><p>Keep the existing library compile limits: 65,536 UTF-8 source bytes, 64 functions, 16 parameters, 256 lets per function, 4,096 visited expressions, 1,024 call sites, depth 64 and call-graph depth 32. Enforce the shared foundation's existing cancellation and resource checks.</p><p>Propose a source String maximum of 1,048,576 UTF-16 code units per value. A host may lower this bound; it cannot increase the qualified profile's hard bound. Validate entry arguments before invoking the body. Check append result length before allocation using exact intermediates or a proved non-overflowing bound check. Validate results before returning them. Store the selected bound in the immutable lowering/runtime contract, not an unauthenticated metadata instruction.</p><p>This is a per-value bound. Existing evaluator fuel and provider deadlines/memory isolation remain necessary for cumulative work; this design does not claim a total allocation cap or preemptible parsing. Test lower configured limits, concatenation at/over the cap, cancellation and repeated calls. Resource refusals remain distinguishable from malformed text and unsupported operations.</p></section><section id="delivery"><h2>Implementation contracts and shared ownership</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Bead</th><th>Result</th><th>Dependency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.12.3</td><td>Shared typed signatures/checking/lowering and correctly owned metadata, with Bool/Int32 source-to-consumer proof</td><td>Closed compiler pin; coordinate existing active N1 handoff</td></tr><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.2.1</td><td>String/Bool basics, explicit boundary helpers and runnable MoonBit workbench sample</td><td>Reviewed design + shared typed foundation</td></tr><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.2.2</td><td>Shortlex Boolean comparisons with exact Unicode semantics</td><td>String basics</td></tr><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.2.3</td><td>Int32 length and owned, pair-safe slices</td><td>String basics + N1 qualification</td></tr><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.2.4</td><td>Four pinned predicates through the shared immutable resolver</td><td>String basics + shared resolver .1.10.12.4</td></tr><tr><td>morphir-ukvn.1.10.13.2.5</td><td>Combined profile, installed consumers and complete evidence matrix</td><td>Four completed String implementation slices; no N6 blocker</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Preserve N1's in-progress issue, ownership, existing SDK/invocation handoff and qualification scope. The published handoff contains SDK/invocation work, not an implemented typed frontend. Coordinate shared type/schema/resolver edits before implementation; do not build a competing String parser or resolver. The shared milestones may land with the original numeric slices while retaining separate evidence.</p><details><summary>Proposed source-to-consumer flow</summary><p>The evaluator/generated branches need independent expected original-source rows. <a href="../strings-source-design/diagrams/qualification.html">Open the explorable flow</a>.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 822 528" data-layout-contract="readable-v2" role="img" lang="en" aria-labelledby="archify-diagram-title archify-diagram-description" data-animation="trace" data-preset="classic" data-quality-profile="showcase" data-node-ui="" data-view-scale="1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
