Add a recording mode to `Reader`, and change `parse_program` to build the reader with `record: None`:

```moonbit
///|
pub(all) struct Located {
  datum : Datum
  start : Int
  children : Array[Located]
} derive(Eq, Debug)

///|
priv struct Reader {
  chars : Array[Char]
  mut index : Int
  // Pre-order start offsets of every datum read, when recording.
  record : Array[Int]?
}
```

- In `Reader::read`, right after `self.skip(depth)` and the end-of-input check, push `self.index` when `record` is
  `Some(r)`. This is the datum's start.
- For the quote sugar `'x`, push the same offset once more, for the synthetic `Symbol("quote")`, before reading `x`.
  This keeps the pre-order list aligned with the produced `Sequence([quote, x])`.
- In `Reader::skip`, for a datum comment `#;`, note `r.length()` before `ignore(self.read(depth + 1))` and
  `truncate` back to it afterwards. Ignored datums must leave no offsets.

Then:

```moonbit
///|
fn attach(datum : Datum, offsets : Array[Int], next : Ref[Int]) -> Located {
  let start = offsets[next.val]
  next.val += 1
  let children = match datum {
    Sequence(items, tail) => {
      let result = items.map(item => attach(item, offsets, next))
      if tail is Some(t) {
        result.push(attach(t, offsets, next))
      }
      result
    }
    Vector(items) => items.map(item => attach(item, offsets, next))
    _ => []
  }
  { datum, start, children }
}

///|
pub fn parse_program_located(source : String) -> Array[Located] raise {
  let offsets : Array[Int] = []
  let reader = Reader::{ chars: source.iter().collect(), index: 0, record: Some(offsets) }
  let forms = []
  reader.skip(0)
  while reader.index < reader.chars.length() {
    forms.push(reader.read(0))
    reader.skip(0)
  }
  let next = Ref::new(0)
  forms.map(form => attach(form, offsets, next))
}

///|
pub fn line_column(source : String, offset : Int) -> (Int, Int) {
  let mut line = 1
  let mut column = 1
  for i, c in source.iter().collect() {
    if i >= offset {
      break
    }
    if c == '\n' {
      line += 1
      column = 1
    } else {
      column += 1
    }
  }
  (line, column)
}
```

A vector `#( … )` starts at its `#`. The vector is read through the inner `read` of `(`, so the reader pushes two
offsets for it. Drop the inner one by replacing it: for `#(`, record the `#` offset, then read the inner list
without recording it (pop the extra entry after the inner read). Cover this with a test: `(#(a b))`.

