}</code></pre></details></section><section id="pipeline"><div class="section-no">03 / A7</div><h2>Give each phase a precise input and output</h2><figure class="diagram" id="a7-pipeline"><div class="diagram-render"></div><figcaption>Proposed library compiler. Declaration collection, lexical resolution and a checked module are new phase contracts. The resulting IRUnit rejoins the landed engine. A rejection prevents publication of the entire source unit.</figcaption><details><summary>Mermaid source</summary><pre><code class="mermaid-source">flowchart TB
S[One immutable file and logical module identity] --> P[Pinned parser adapter]
P --> D[Collect all declared Boolean signatures]
D --> R[Resolve lexical bindings and direct calls]
R --> C[Check Boolean bodies, arity and acyclic call graph]
C -->|Accepted checked module| L[Lower typed references, applications and lets]
P -->|Syntax or recovery error| X[Structured diagnostic; no source unit]
R -->|Unknown binding| X
C -->|Unsupported or invalid model| X
L --> U[Typed IRUnit with native Ion origins]
U --> I[Existing checkpoint codecs: Ion preferred, JSON supported]
U --> G[Existing generator and build provider]
G --> E[Existing execution and Scheme comparison]
E --> V[Verified generated library]
</code></pre></details></figure><p><a href=".assets/a7-library-20261004/library-phases.html">Explore the proposed phases as an SVG diagram</a>. The viewer is standalone and works offline. Each phase is a pure in-memory transformation; the host owns source reads, compiler execution and artifact publication.</p><p>The current seed compiler combines body checking and lowering. The library compiler separates them through internal typed views. This is a proposal, not a claim that the landed compiler already has a resolver or a reusable inference service.</p></section><section id="contracts"><div class="section-no">04 / A7</div><h2>Compose pure phases around a rich source boundary</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase or owner</th><th>Input</th><th>Output and responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Source host</td><td>Selected path and explicit logical identity</td><td>One immutable source snapshot and bounded text. No hidden filesystem, registry or process calls in the frontend.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parser adapter</td><td>Text and pinned parser identity</td><td>Frontend-owned syntax view with source locations; fail on syntax reports or recovered nodes. Keep upstream AST types private to the adapter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Declaration collector</td><td>Syntax module and selected profile</td><td>DeclaredModule containing every unique function signature, visibility and origin. Collect before checking bodies so forward calls are legal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resolver</td><td>DeclaredModule and lexical bodies</td><td>ResolvedModule with distinct binding identities and direct call targets. Maintain ordered lexical scopes; record the complete call graph.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profile checker</td><td>ResolvedModule and Boolean profile rules</td><td>CheckedModule with body types, typed calls and acyclic graph. Validate all declarations, including private or unreachable ones. No general Hindley-Milner inference in this slice.</td></tr>
<tr><td>IR lowerer</td><td>CheckedModule</td><td>One typed current Library IR plus native Ion annotations, node origins and provenance. No guessed types, placeholders or inlining.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engine adapter</td><td>LibraryOutput, selected language/provider/profile and host-owned unit ID</td><td>IRUnit through the existing source callback envelope. Core validates identity, metadata ownership and budgets.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Generator/build/execution</td><td>Existing typed IR contracts</td><td>Reuse E1/E2 providers, entry manifests, dependency pins, bounded leases and comparison. Compiler acceptance remains a host capability.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><h3>Add a library API without changing the seed API</h3><p>Keep <code>compile(Input, Limits, ...)</code>, its <code>Output.entry</code>/<code>Output.public</code>, default profile and accepted language unchanged. Propose additive <code>compile_library</code>, <code>LibraryLimits</code> and <code>LibraryOutput</code>. LibraryOutput retains unit ID, current IR, metadata, annotations and provenance, and exposes a canonically ordered list of public entry FQNames. The generated execution manifest remains the authority for invokable entries and their codecs. Do not choose the first declaration as an implicit entry.</p><p>One file produces one logical public module with each declaration's original visibility. An all-private library may compile and generate with an empty public-entry list. An execution request for a private or absent entry fails explicitly. No source package dependencies or module merges are implied by this contract.</p><h3>Keep composition configurable without spreading language policy</h3><p>The parser, collector, resolver, checker and lowerer have named pure contracts and are composed by the selected provider/profile for the MoonBit language. Start with internal implementations and tests. If hosts later supply alternate implementations, require explicit contract/profile identities and the same conformance suite; replacing a phase cannot silently weaken admission. Applications choose and register the adapter. Engine core, the generator and host providers remain independent of MoonBit syntax.</p><p>Reuse the existing source compilation callback and IRUnit envelope. Extend registration and planning to distinguish language, provider and profile; the current flat registration name cannot represent those independently. Adapt existing registrations as default selections rather than renaming languages to avoid a registry collision. Preserve the rich-unit loss guards at JSON component boundaries. Declaration, local-binding and call-site origins live in native Ion metadata; semantic typing belongs in current IR attributes. The pure compiler records source language and semantic profile; the host adds the resolved provider identity and capability evidence to its receipt. Preserve the seed provenance field named frontend with its existing profile-valued meaning. New records distinguish those concepts explicitly instead of silently reinterpreting historical metadata.</p></section><section id="profile"><div class="section-no">05 / A7</div><h2>Grow the seed into a Boolean library</h2><p>The landed <code>moonbit-model-bool-v1</code> accepts exactly one function with explicit Boolean parameters and result. It rejects calls and local bindings. The proposed <code>moonbit-model-bool-library-v1</code> is opt-in and preserves those Boolean operator rules.</p><pre><code>fn ready(active : Bool, suspended : Bool) -&gt; Bool {
  active &amp;&amp; !suspended
}

pub fn eligible(active : Bool, suspended : Bool, vip : Bool) -&gt; Bool {
  let allowed : Bool = ready(active, suspended)
  allowed &amp;&amp; vip
}

pub fn default_eligible() -&gt; Bool {
  eligible(true, false, true)
}</code></pre><p>This is the proposed acceptance seed. The <a href=".assets/a7-library-20261004/source-probe.json">original-source design probe</a> compiles this exact source and checks eight eligibility combinations plus the zero-argument result on Wasm, Wasm GC, JS and native in debug and release. The library frontend is not implemented; original-source execution alone does not prove lowering. The public functions are selectable entries; the private helper stays callable inside its module and inaccessible to external consumers.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Library profile decision</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Top-level declarations</td><td>One or more ordinary functions</td><td>Explicit named positional Bool parameters and Bool return. Preserve plain pub exports and default, unexported functions. Reject explicit priv syntax, overloads and duplicate names.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Function calls</td><td>Direct, fully applied calls within the file</td><td>Unqualified declared target, exact arity, Boolean arguments, Boolean result. Forward calls allowed. Zero-argument calls supported. No function values, partial applications, labelled/optional parameters or qualified calls.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Immutable bindings</td><td>Sequential let bindings with one named binder</td><td>Optional Bool annotation; initializer checked as Bool. Initializer sees the previous scope, and the new binder is visible only in the remaining body. Shadowing uses a new binding identity. Reject destructuring and local function declarations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expressions</td><td>Existing Boolean expressions plus calls and lets</td><td>Literals, lexical references, grouping, Bool constraints, if/else, !, &amp;&amp;, ||, == and !=. Require a Boolean final expression; no arbitrary discarded statement expressions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call graph</td><td>Acyclic</td><td>Reject direct and indirect recursion across every declaration, even in unreachable branches. Compute cycles without recursive host-stack traversal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Types and effects</td><td>Same restricted pure language</td><td>No numeric/string/compound types, generics, traits, mutation, async, exceptions, FFI, loops or early return.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Packages</td><td>One file, one module</td><td>No imports, cross-file calls, dependency discovery or multi-document linking. Existing engine jobs remain independent.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><div class="callout open"><strong>Compiler-validity finding</strong><p>Compiler <code>0.10.14+7d59c7ec9</code> rejects <code>priv fn</code> with diagnostic <code>3005</code>, "No 'private' visibility for function." Default <code>fn</code> supplies the unexported helper used here. The landed seed adapter accepts the parser's <code>Priv</code> visibility variant. That admission mismatch needs a focused correctness fix and regression gate; compatibility does not require preserving compiler-invalid input. This revision changes no production code.</p></div><h3>Make names and evaluation order explicit</h3><p>Collect the full declaration table before resolving any body. Resolve a name in the current lexical scope first. A Boolean local or parameter that shadows a function makes a call to that spelling a non-callable-target error. A bare function reference is unsupported; only direct call syntax admits it. Duplicate parameters fail. Repeated let spellings may shadow earlier bindings and must never overwrite their IR identity.</p><p>Retain the injective UTF-8 encoding for global names. Assign a distinct deterministic binder identity per declaration and lexical binding position, with separate namespaces for source bindings and lowering temporaries. Record the original spelling and source span in Ion. Formatting can change coordinates; this is not a promise of stable node identity across arbitrary source edits.</p><p>Lower zero-input locals to strict <code>LetDefinition</code> values. Evaluate a binding initializer once before its body. Lower direct calls to typed <code>Reference</code>/<code>Apply</code> nodes, with curried function types on references and intermediate applications, and Bool on the final result. A zero-argument function uses its zero-input value reference. Preserve function/argument order and short circuiting. Do not inline callees or expand the call graph into expression trees.</p><p>The existing generator already lowers zero-input local definitions to eager values and introduces application temporaries. The library gate must verify the emitted result and the Scheme path, rather than infer source equivalence from those implementation details alone.</p><h3>Use bounded compilation, not implied bounded evaluation</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Proposed hard maximum</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>65,536 UTF-8 bytes; 64 declarations</td><td>Whole source unit</td></tr><tr><td>16 parameters; 256 let binders</td><td>Each function</td></tr><tr><td>4,096 visited source expressions; 1,024 call sites</td><td>Aggregate across all bodies</td></tr><tr><td>Expression depth 64; call-graph depth 32</td><td>Checked before publication, including private declarations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>LibraryLimits may lower these maxima. Check cancellation between phases and during bounded traversals. Choose the first primary diagnostic deterministically by source order, with a source span where available. Failure publishes no partial unit. The synchronous parser still needs host isolation for a hard interruption/allocation guarantee. An acyclic graph can perform substantial repeated work, so execution continues to use its own deadlines and budgets.</p><h3>Broader source support follows separate evidence</h3><p>Records and custom types should reuse language-neutral IR typing where appropriate. Shared inference is tracked as <code>morphir-ukvn.5.22.2</code>; this explicitly annotated Boolean library does not depend on it. MoonBit-specific numeric widths, overflow, conversions, strings, effects and recursion each need their own source semantics and independent compiler acceptance. Never silently map machine Int arithmetic to exact Morphir Integer arithmetic.</p></section><section id="ion"><div class="section-no">06 / A7</div><h2>Use Ion internally and preserve JSON compatibility</h2><p>Source files remain MoonBit text. The parser's in-memory AST is not a generic JSON intermediate. The accepted frontend output is typed IR inside the existing IRUnit envelope; checkpoints default to binary Ion and can explicitly use Ion text.</p><p>Attach native Ion metadata for source spans, original names and profile identity to stable declaration/body IDs. Preserve unknown annotation tokens and source provenance across components. Version the coordinate convention and record the source digest; do not assume parser offsets equal UTF-8 byte offsets. Verify coordinates with non-ASCII and multiline fixtures before claiming exact mapping.</p><p>Morphir JSON remains supported for semantic IR and downstream tools. A JSON boundary must use the existing loss-detection or metadata sidecar policy. Source origins and rich Ion values must not vanish in a generic JSON conversion. JSON and JSON Lines CLI result framing remain distinct from internal pipeline storage.</p><p>A future cache key includes source bytes, logical package/module identity, parser/source pin, semantic profile, dependency inventory, lowering version and relevant budgets. Cache hits require complete verified evidence. No cache is necessary for the first slice.</p></section><section id="host"><div class="section-no">07 / A7</div><h2>Keep capabilities and instrumentation explicit</h2><p>Parsing and lowering have no host I/O. Hosts supply snapshots and enforce input-byte limits before parsing. Bound AST size/depth, declarations, diagnostic count and generated artifacts; traversal limits do not prove a streaming or pre-allocation parser guarantee. Cancellation is checked at stage boundaries and bounded traversals. A synchronous parser cannot promise prompt interruption during a long parse; hostile-input isolation needs a separate worker or process capability.</p><p>The landed observation slice <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.6</code> reuses the portable contract. The pure frontend accepts an optional scope with a disabled default. A frontend span contains parse, declaration profile-check and expression checking/lowering spans. The Boolean profile has no import resolver; do not report a resolve stage it does not perform. The engine adapter accepts a host-owned observer. CLI <code>--log</code> enables the existing bounded local sink with automatic directories. Source compilation defaults to <code>.morphir/logs/frontend.ionb</code> under the discovered project/workspace; execute, verify and conform default to <code>.morphir/logs/execution.ionb</code> under cwd. Explicit <code>--log-file</code> paths take precedence and remain unchanged, including outside that folder. No extra escape rejection is imposed. The default sink uses binary Ion and also allows Ion text, JSON Lines or text, with matching filename extensions. Creation runs only in the bounded flush worker. Standalone WASI/Wasm GC CLI hosts retain their explicit clock-capability rejection for requested local delivery; pure host-supplied observation and normal source compilation still work. Dry-run uses a disabled observer and does not open logs. Frontend cancellation must end active spans as cancelled while preserving its original diagnostic. Sink failure must leave model outputs, metadata/provenance and diagnostics unchanged. Disabled observation performs no clock, ID or sink work. Record terminal outcomes and durations with bounded dimensions such as frontend/profile/stage/outcome. Keep source text, argument values and diagnostic free text out of default telemetry attributes. Required diagnostics and execution evidence are never sampled away.</p><p>Local Ion logs, stderr text and explicit JSON Lines sinks remain available. The native OpenTelemetry adapter is opt-in with binary, pin, helper and endpoint; supported HTTP/JSON host coverage stays Linux/x64 and macOS/arm64. Pure frontend packages never import the native exporter. Missing required capability fails planning.</p><h3>Instrument real library phases</h3><p>For the proposed library compiler, a frontend span contains parse, declaration profile-check, resolve, body profile-check and lower spans. Add a portable <code>Resolve</code> stage with stable name <code>resolve</code>; the existing seed keeps its current stage sequence. Distinguish the two profile-check phases with a fixed, bounded phase dimension. Do not emit per-function names, graph edges, source snippets or node metadata as default telemetry.</p><p>Every started phase has one success, failure or cancellation terminal event. Resolution/type failures retain the original source diagnostic. Cancellation must not return a partially checked module or IRUnit. Disabled observation and optional sink failures retain the existing neutrality guarantees. Reuse source <code>.morphir/logs/frontend.ionb</code> defaults and explicit path overrides; no new log directory or exporter is needed.</p></section><section id="cli"><div class="section-no">08 / A7</div><h2>Keep MoonBit the language as support grows</h2><p>Users choose <code>moonbit</code>. The selected provider reports the source subset it supports, the profile in use and the capabilities behind it. Multiple declarations and calls are a gain in MoonBit support, not a new language.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th><th>MoonBit example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Language</td><td>The language of the source documents</td><td><code>moonbit</code>, with <code>.mbt</code> sources</td></tr>
<tr><td>Provider</td><td>The implementation compiling that language</td><td>The built-in MoonBit source provider; future installed providers have their own identities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profile</td><td>A versioned source subset and lowering contract</td><td><code>moonbit-model-bool-v1</code> or <code>moonbit-model-bool-library-v1</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Advertised operations, accepted source behavior and IR support</td><td>Boolean signatures, multiple declarations, local bindings and direct acyclic calls; one document, no incremental compilation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Checks supporting particular claims</td><td>Original compiler/IR/generator differential receipts; MCK verification only after the relevant extension suite passes</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><h3>Follow the existing ecosystem distinction</h3><ul><li><a href=".assets/a7-ecosystem-20261004/two-elm-providers.md">Two Elm frontend providers</a> keeps <code>elm</code> as the language while selecting a provider separately.</li><li><a href=".assets/a7-ecosystem-20261004/ir-capability-sets.md">Extensions declare IR capability sets</a> assigns feature vocabulary to the IR specification and verification to operation-specific MCK cases.</li><li><a href=".assets/a7-ecosystem-20261004/protocol.md">MEP capability negotiation</a> separates extension identity from <code>frontend.languages</code> and requires the host to consult claims on each run.</li></ul><p>The local engine currently stores a flat frontend name, one profile and dependency pins. It does not yet implement this full selection model. The following profile setting and flag are proposed additions, not existing CLI features:</p><pre><code>[frontend]
language = "moonbit"
# Optional reproducibility constraint; proposed new setting:
profile = "moonbit-model-bool-library-v1"

[pipeline]
backend = "checkpoint"

# Existing language selection keeps its meaning:
morphir run . --frontend moonbit --backend checkpoint --json

# Proposed optional profile selection:
morphir run . --frontend moonbit --frontend-profile moonbit-model-bool-library-v1 \
  --backend moonbit --validation required --json-lines</code></pre><h3>Resolve the selection before compiling</h3><ol><li>Select source language from explicit configuration or discovery.</li><li>Resolve an eligible provider using advertised language/operation capabilities and explicit provider policy. Report ambiguity rather than choosing by registration order.</li><li>Choose the requested profile, or the selected provider's declared default. Keep the existing seed default for this additive slice; any later default promotion needs an explicit compatibility decision.</li><li>Validate profile availability, requested capabilities and host budgets. Freeze the provider, profile, dependency identities and effective claims in the plan.</li><li>Compile through that profile's callback and validate its resulting IRUnit before downstream stages.</li></ol><p>One MoonBit provider can advertise both profiles and route a captured selection to the existing seed compiler or the additive library compiler. Each project resolves independently, so mixed-profile workspaces need no mutable global profile or second language alias. Unknown profiles fail before source reads, naming the provider and available choices. Never silently widen a request or fall back to another subset.</p><h3>Show capabilities without making users memorize profile IDs</h3><p>Human planning output shows <strong>MoonBit · Boolean library · one file</strong>, with a short supported/unsupported summary. JSON and JSON Lines carry distinct language, provider identity, selected profile, default-profile status, dependencies, output contract, effective limits and capability evidence status. This is proposed descriptor behavior, not the current SourceFrontendSelection shape.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Capability group</th><th>Seed profile, landed</th><th>Library profile, proposed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Source language</td><td>MoonBit</td><td>MoonBit</td></tr><tr><td>Function declarations</td><td>Exactly one</td><td>One or more, within profile limits</td></tr><tr><td>Immutable local bindings</td><td>Rejected</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><td>Direct calls</td><td>Rejected</td><td>Fully applied, same-file and acyclic</td></tr><tr><td>Source types</td><td>Explicit Bool signatures</td><td>Explicit Bool signatures; checked Bool locals</td></tr><tr><td>Documents / incremental</td><td>One / unavailable</td><td>One / unavailable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Build these summaries from the selected profile descriptor and checked claims, not parallel hard-coded CLI lists. Profile contracts also carry source-language restrictions that do not map directly to IR features. Use the IR-owned vocabulary for emitted/accepted IR support; do not invent a second per-extension feature taxonomy.</p><p>The inspected ecosystem documents establish language/capability separation, but do not standardize the profile selector or the exact fine-grained feature wire shape proposed here. Those local contracts need implementation and must align with the eventual shared vocabulary before F4 publication. Local evidence, artifact claim probing and MCK semantic verification remain distinct. Unknown or unverified compatibility must be visible; runtime source checking and generation validation still enforce the declared subset.</p><p>Dry-run reads no model bodies, discovers no entries and creates no logs. It reports the captured selection and known capabilities. Source-dependent unsupported constructs remain compiler diagnostics naming the selected provider/profile and source span. A larger source set fails when the provider declares single-document compilation.</p><p>The existing explicit compiler provider remains required for build validation. A source directory with several files still yields independent units; this profile does not enable linking. After compilation, use the existing execute/verify path and explicitly select a public entry from the generated manifest. Convenience commands and upstream Rust CLI adoption remain separate work.</p></section><section id="acceptance"><div class="section-no">09 / A7</div><h2>Prove library semantics against original source</h2><figure class="diagram" id="a7-acceptance"><div class="diagram-render"></div><figcaption>The library gate covers every declared public entry in each fixture. Original compiler execution, hand-authored expectations, Scheme and generated execution must agree. Host receipts distinguish actual execution from pure compiler tests.</figcaption><details><summary>Mermaid source</summary><pre><code class="mermaid-source">sequenceDiagram
participant H as Acceptance host
participant M as Pinned MoonBit compiler
participant F as Pure library frontend
participant S as Scheme evaluator
participant G as E1/E2 generated execution
H->>M: Compile exact original library and test consumer visibility
M-->>H: Public entry results and compiler identities
H->>F: Same immutable file and selected library profile
F-->>H: Typed Library IR, public entries and Ion origins
loop Every public entry and complete fixture truth table
H->>S: Evaluate lowered entry with identical arguments
S-->>H: Boolean result
H->>G: Invoke required generated target
G-->>H: Boolean result and identity receipt
H->>H: Compare original, independent expectation, Scheme and generated result
end
</code></pre></details></figure><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Required evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Profile semantics</td><td>Multiple declarations, forward calls, private helpers, zero-argument calls, nested lets, shadowing and short circuiting. Fixtures have bounded Boolean parameter sets; exhaust all combinations for every public entry and record expected row counts before running.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rejection</td><td>Duplicate declarations/parameters, unknown names, local non-callable shadows, wrong arity/type, bare function references, direct/indirect cycles, unsupported imports/effects/types, missing final expressions and recovered syntax. Type-invalid source must also be rejected by the original compiler. Valid but excluded MoonBit must get a profile rejection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Visibility and typing</td><td>Separate consumers can call public functions and cannot name private helpers. All-private libraries have no public entry. Assert reference/intermediate application types, unique local binders and metadata ownership. Verify no call-tree expansion; Boolean outcomes alone do not prove evaluation order or bounded lowering.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compatibility</td><td>Keep the existing seed API, stage sequence and byte-identical canonical IR/metadata receipts for valid unchanged seed fixtures. Preserve diagnostics except where a separately reviewed compiler-validity correction is required. Language selection remains moonbit; optional profile selection and capability summaries identify the accepted subset. Current and supported historical Morphir JSON inputs still work in downstream generator gates.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ion and JSON boundaries</td><td>Ion text/binary checkpoints preserve all entry types, origins and provenance. The existing explicit JSON-only component retains the native rich envelope. Lossy Morphir JSON export must fail explicitly or use a documented supported policy; do not silently strip library metadata.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observation and cancellation</td><td>Phase parentage and exactly one terminal outcome on success/failure/cancellation. No-op performs no clock/ID/sink work. Sink failures do not change IR, diagnostics or checkpoints. Dry-run reads no source bodies and creates no files.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limits and determinism</td><td>Boundary/over-bound cases for bytes, declarations, aggregate expressions, call sites, lets and graph depth. Stable source-order diagnostics and canonical public-entry ordering; cancellations during collection, resolution, graph checks and lowering. Do not compare duration or random trace IDs as deterministic artifacts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Installed execution</td><td>Clean working directory and packed npm package. Required JS, Wasm GC and native C lanes in debug/release, plus separately pinned LLVM on supported darwin/arm64. Required lanes fail when unavailable; compile, invoke all public entries, compare, dispose and retain identities.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>Pure tests and CLI compatibility checks run on all existing MoonBit targets, including WASI. Preserve the current local-logging capability limits on standalone WASI/Wasm GC hosts. Local and hosted required lanes retain source, parser, profile, compiler/core, SDK, runtime and invocation identities. A completed local library gate does not prove upstream extension installation.</p></section><section id="delivery"><div class="section-no">10 / A7</div><h2>Deliver small, reviewable slices</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Slice</th><th>Concrete result</th><th>Gate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F0, this study</td><td>Pinned parser probe, semantic risks, metadata-hook design and HTML diagrams.</td><td>Six tests on four targets, compiler type-error check, offline HTML QA. No frontend implementation claim.</td></tr><tr><td>F1, Boolean library</td><td>Pure parser adapter, profile checker and one-function typed IR lowering.</td><td>Truth-table comparison against original source and Scheme; malformed/unsupported input rejection.</td></tr><tr><td>F2, engine registration</td><td>Metadata-aware source contract, legacy frontend adaptation, CLI configuration and Ion checkpoints.</td><td>Preserve origins through codecs/components, dry-run and all output modes; no regressions to existing frontends.</td></tr><tr><td>F3, installed execution</td><td>Boolean source model generated and executed through landed E1/E2.</td><td>Required supported target matrix, bounded cleanup, installed package receipts and instrumentation neutrality.</td></tr><tr><td>F4, A2-dependent packaging</td><td>Publish the agreed extension manifest/bundle and verify host installation.</td><td>Wait for A2 contract; independent upstream Rust and component acceptance, if selected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Next local library slices</h3><p>These are proposed review boundaries, not executed tasks or a claim of implementation. The library acceptance fixture should exercise the whole path before broader source support begins.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Proposed slice</th><th>Result</th><th>Landing gate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>L1, pure library compiler</td><td>Add the opt-in library API, pure phase contracts, multi-function resolution, Boolean checking, acyclic graph validation and typed lowering.</td><td>All-target focused tests, original compiler differential fixtures, limits, scope/visibility and seed compatibility. The full advertised profile must pass before registration.</td></tr><tr><td>L2, adapter and CLI</td><td>Separate language, provider and profile selection in generic registration/planning; expose both profiles under moonbit with capability summaries and public-entry behavior.</td><td>Lazy profile selection, mixed-profile projects, claim-driven capabilities and named rejection; Ion/JSON preservation, build validation, multi-entry execution and neutral observations.</td></tr><tr><td>L3, installed qualification</td><td>Run original/Scheme/generated library parity from the installed CLI on required targets and hosts.</td><td>All public entry rows, debug/release, pinned LLVM where supported, consumer privacy, failed sinks, cancellation/cleanup and identity receipts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Separate dependencies</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Work</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Next requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Boolean library, L1-L3</td><td>No A2 or shared-inference dependency</td><td>Proceed with the pure compiler, adapter/CLI and installed qualification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Richer source typing</td><td>Shared inference<br><code>morphir-ukvn.5.22.2</code></td><td>Coordinate reusable IR typing and define MoonBit-specific semantics.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Multi-file linking</td><td>A separate project-compilation design</td><td><ul><li>Complete host-owned source snapshot</li><li>Public interfaces and dependency inventory</li><li>Unit aggregation and deterministic module graph</li></ul></td></tr>
<tr><td>F4 extension delivery<br><code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.5</code></td><td>A2 packaging contract<br><code>morphir-ukvn.1.3</code></td><td>Agree the bundle and guest boundary. Verify install, describe, initialize, compile and generate in an independently pinned upstream host.</td></tr>
<tr><td>WIT component route</td><td>B2 contracts and B3 host support, both open</td><td>Prove component compatibility through host acceptance. Keep Extism compatibility for existing installs.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p class="footnote">Extension delivery preserves Ion internally and uses an explicit checked transport boundary. A native or WASI build does not establish component compatibility.</p><p>Numeric, compound-type, package-linking and effect profiles follow as separately scoped work after F1-F3. A7 remains open until its source-model and extension-delivery acceptance are satisfied. F1 is tracked as <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.2</code>. Its pure package and compiler gate landed in <a href="https://github.com/finos/morphir-moonbit/pull/38">PR #38</a>, squash commit <code>2e8f8e50ae0f6a5b7540495fe42245fc21304ac7</code>, with all 20 checks passing. The compiler gate compares 11 original models and generated libraries against 44 independently expected Boolean outcomes and Scheme on JS, native, Wasm and Wasm GC in debug and release. It also found and fixed nested-condition parentheses in the existing generator. F2 landed in <a href="https://github.com/finos/morphir-moonbit/pull/40">PR #40</a>, squash <code>32f2033d86ddffe98735bed3d407a2540f6f23a1</code>, after all 20 checks passed and no unresolved review threads remained, on <code>DamianReeves/morphir-a7-engine</code> and tracked as <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.3</code>. Its focused engine/adapter tests pass 28 cases on each of four targets in debug and release. Full workspace tests pass 723 Wasm, 723 Wasm GC, 730 JS and 729 native cases. CLI source/checkpoint/generation cases pass on all four hosts and the installed npm package. Required lint, format and package validation gates pass. Compiler-provider regression passes, including 60 execution fault cases and rich pricing parity on JS, Wasm GC and native in debug/release. F3 landed in <a href="https://github.com/finos/morphir-moonbit/pull/41">PR #41</a>, squash <code>b1a02987806bc6e58d8800f7b4bb823b2fbd693c</code>, after all 19 exact-head checks passed and no unresolved review threads remained, tracked as <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.4</code>. It extends installed npm acceptance with CLI source lowering, binary Ion checkpoints and four Boolean rows compared against the original compiler, independent expectations, Scheme and generated execution. Required lanes include JS, Wasm GC and native C, plus hash-pinned LLVM on darwin/arm64, in debug and release. The full installed gate passes all four required targets in debug and release. Its 24 existing model/format lanes pass 23 cases each; the source model passes four Boolean rows in eight lanes against the original compiler, independent expectations, Scheme and generated execution. Instrumentation neutrality, failed optional sinks, dry-run, framing, mismatch rejection and lease disposal pass. An additional LLVM-only run verifies configurable target selection and explicitly observed sink failure. Repository lint, format and package validation pass. F4 remains A2-blocked. Independent observation follow-up <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.6</code> landed in PR #43. It adds portable frontend scopes and <code>--log</code> defaults with explicit path overrides. Source/checkpoint privacy, failure/cancellation terminals and sink neutrality pass. Installed acceptance passes 24 existing model/format lanes and eight source lanes on JS, Wasm GC, native C and pinned LLVM in debug/release; 60 execution faults pass. CLI checks pass native, Node, Wasm, Wasm GC and installed npm. The HTML passes offline checks across four widths and both themes. The follow-up landed in <a href="https://github.com/finos/morphir-moonbit/pull/43">PR #43</a>, squash commit <code>1206065c333b4f11d69130f17cd4d94c54c437e5</code>, merged 2026-10-04T16:43:33Z after maintainer approval. Its reviewed head was <code>f805ae0ea228d01de414e32bbcb6171202076e1f</code>, rebased onto <code>40587e6</code>. Final workspace tests pass 737 Wasm, 737 Wasm GC, 746 JS and 745 native. All 20 checks pass on that head, CI run <code>37215900387</code>, with no unresolved review threads. Installed CI receipts verify six Linux source lanes and eight macOS lanes including pinned LLVM, all four frontend stages and identical binary checkpoint identities across hosts. <a href="../a7-observe-ci/acceptance.json">Local CI acceptance receipt</a> retains the checks. The task is closed with merge and validation evidence. A7 remains open; F4 extension delivery still depends on A2. This design is stored in beads.</p></section><section id="decisions"><div class="section-no">11 / A7</div><h2>Fix the first library scope before implementation</h2><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D1</div><div><h3>Separate language, provider and profile</h3><p>Keep <code>moonbit</code> as the language. Propose <code>moonbit-model-bool-library-v1</code> as an optional profile selection under the same provider, with advertised capabilities and evidence status. Keep the existing seed default and API unchanged. The source is one file and one logical module, with all functions checked before publication.</p></div></div><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D2</div><div><h3>Allow direct calls and immutable locals, reject cycles</h3><p>Collect explicit signatures first, resolve lexical names with distinct binder identities, then check fully applied Boolean calls and the whole call graph. Preserve forward calls, local shadowing and same-module private access. No imports, recursion, function values or general inference.</p></div></div><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D3</div><div><h3>Produce a library without an implicit entry</h3><p>Add LibraryOutput with a canonical list of public entries. Reuse typed IR and generated manifest contracts. Private-only source can compile; requests to execute private or absent entries fail. Do not break the seed's single-entry output.</p></div></div><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D4</div><div><h3>Retain pins and prove every claimed construct</h3><p>Keep parser 0.4.1 and matching compiler/core acceptance identities. The design probe verifies the proposed source syntax against the compiler; full differential acceptance remains implementation work. Conformance must cover the full advertised profile, all public entry rows, generated visibility and all required host lanes.</p></div></div><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D5</div><div><h3>Reuse shared typing only when the profile needs it</h3><p>The Boolean checker assigns known Bool/function types and does not wait for <code>morphir-ukvn.5.22.2</code>. Richer types and generic inference get a separate design with that reusable service. Width/overflow semantics remain source-specific responsibilities.</p></div></div><div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D6</div><div><h3>Keep extension delivery independently tracked</h3><p>F4 depends on A2's common layout and packaging command. The roadmap selects WIT/component hosts; B2/B3 establish those contracts. Current upstream packaging evidence uses Extism claims and is not proof that a MoonBit WASI artifact is compatible. Transport must use an explicit checked boundary around rich Ion metadata. A7 remains open; release/publication requires its own authorization.</p></div></div><div class="callout open"><strong>Implementation checks still to resolve</strong><p>Define typed language/provider/profile descriptors and claim-driven selection without a language alias; align fine-grained support with the shared vocabulary. Audit the pinned parser's let/call AST forms and source coordinates, and check zero-argument/unexported-helper lowering across Scheme and the generator. If a required construct fails, report it and revise its admission rule before advertising the profile. The budgets above are proposed hard caps, not measured performance claims.</p></div></section><section id="sources"><div class="section-no">12 / A7</div><h2>Evidence and scope</h2><ul class="refs"><li><a href="https://github.com/finos/morphir-moonbit/pull/37">Landed E2, PR #37</a>, main snapshot <code>79d4d05e0fdae00b8dc9a29feee3dd898dcf6694</code>.</li><li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/types.mbt">Current source frontend registration</a>, lines 79-82 and 172-178; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/plan.mbt">planned pipeline</a>, lines 406-495, wraps legacy IRFile and routes rich units.</li><li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/data/types.mbt">IRUnit metadata and provenance</a>; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/data/registry.mbt">Ion defaults and Morphir JSON loss guards</a>.</li><li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/generation.mbt">Generator unit adapter</a>; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit/README.md">current generator profile and exact-number/UTF-16 mapping</a>.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/moonbitlang/parser">Official parser repository</a>. Published archive 0.4.1 and isolated probe live in <a href="../a7-feasibility/identity.json">local identity receipt</a>.</li><li><a href="../a7-feasibility/probe/probe_test.mbt">Six probe cases</a>; <a href="../a7-feasibility/bad-source/bad.mbt">negative compiler fixture</a>. Compiler help and logs are local evidence, not a public typed-AST contract.</li><li><a href=".assets/a7-ecosystem-20261004/identity.json">Pinned ecosystem evidence</a> retains committed protocol and decisions at <code>5c5307d89f34d876e8f1ebf561ec55efbadf9fa0</code>; the profile selector remains a local proposal. <a href="../../CONTEXT.md">Local glossary</a> records the language/provider/profile distinction.</li><li><a href=".assets/a7-library-20261004/library-phases.finalize-summary.json">Proposed data-flow specification and artifact receipt</a>, with validation, strict artifact checks and browser gate passing; <a href=".assets/a7-library-20261004/library-phases.browser-check.json">browser evidence</a>.</li><li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit-frontend/types.mbt">Seed API and limits</a>; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-engine/source_frontends.mbt">registration, identity validation and lazy descriptors</a>; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit/expressions.mbt">reference, application and local-definition lowering</a>; <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit/lowered_types.mbt">curried expression construction</a>. Evidence snapshot <code>1206065c333b4f11d69130f17cd4d94c54c437e5</code>.</li><li>Beads: <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10</code>, study <code>morphir-ukvn.1.10.1</code>. A2 blocker <code>morphir-ukvn.1.3</code>. Workbench and Rust umbrella work retain separate ownership/completion.</li><li><a href="../a7-f3-installed/moonbit-source/summary.json">Installed source acceptance receipt</a>; <a href="../a7-f3-llvm-config/moonbit-source/summary.json">LLVM-only selection and sink-failure evidence</a>.</li></ul><p class="footnote">Local review artifact. F1 production source is in <a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit-frontend/README.md">the Boolean frontend package</a>. <a href="../frontend-acceptance/identity.json">Compiler acceptance identities</a> and <a href="../frontend-acceptance/models.json">source, IR and generated artifacts</a> record the current implementation. The seed frontend, engine registration, installed source acceptance and observations are landed. Library phases and profile selection in this revision are proposed; A2 extension delivery remains unfinished. Diagram checks and browser QA do not prove semantic correctness.</p></section><footer>Revision 0.10 proposes library slices L1-L3; they are not implemented. F0 is complete; F1, F2 and F3 landed in PRs #38, #40 and #41, and frontend instrumentation landed in PR #43. E2 is merged; A7 remains open and final extension delivery depends on A2.</footer></main></div><script src=".assets/mermaid-12.0.0.min.js"></script><script>window.diagramReady=(async()=>{const dark=matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;mermaid.initialize({startOnLoad:false,securityLevel:'strict',theme:'base',themeVariables:{primaryColor:dark?'#203a2d':'#e8f3ed',primaryTextColor:dark?'#e2eee7':'#172c24',primaryBorderColor:dark?'#72d3a9':'#096348',lineColor:dark?'#b0c0b7':'#53665e',secondaryColor:dark?'#392d20':'#fff3e2',tertiaryColor:dark?'#1c231f':'#fff',actorBkg:dark?'#203a2d':'#e8f3ed',actorTextColor:dark?'#e2eee7':'#172c24',signalColor:dark?'#b0c0b7':'#53665e',signalTextColor:dark?'#e2eee7':'#172c24',noteBkgColor:dark?'#392d20':'#fff3e2',noteTextColor:dark?'#e2eee7':'#172c24'},flowchart:{htmlLabels:false,useMaxWidth:true},sequence:{useMaxWidth:true,wrap:true}});for(const f of document.querySelectorAll('.diagram')){const r=await mermaid.render(f.id+'-svg',f.querySelector('.mermaid-source').textContent);f.querySelector('.diagram-render').innerHTML=r.svg;}document.documentElement.dataset.diagrams='ready';})().catch(e=>{document.documentElement.dataset.diagrams='error';throw e;});</script></body></html>

