<body>
<main style="overflow-wrap:anywhere"><h1>Runtime capabilities: verification and validation</h1><p class="meta">morphir-etg2.6 · 17 October 2026 · planning complete; implementation tracked separately</p><nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents"><ul><li><a href="#vocabulary">Capabilities, verification and validation</a></li><li><a href="#goal">Goal and scope</a></li><li><a href="#status">Evidence and completion</a></li><li><a href="#workflow">Capability validation and execution flow</a></li><li><a href="#policy">Policy and exact negotiation</a></li><li><a href="#boundaries">Checks that always happen</a></li><li><a href="#deferral">Deferral and reached failures</a></li><li><a href="#outcomes">Validate actual results with the validated context</a></li><li><a href="#capture">Capture policy through asynchronous execution</a></li><li><a href="#matrix">Acceptance matrix</a></li><li><a href="#qualification">Upstream contract and qualification</a></li><li><a href="#resources">Measured budgets and SDK limits</a></li><li><a href="#ui">Workbench behavior</a></li><li><a href="#delivery">Implementation sequence</a></li><li><a href="#evidence">Source evidence and limits</a></li></ul></nav><section id="vocabulary"><h2>Capabilities, verification and validation</h2><p><strong>Runtime capabilities</strong> describe what a particular runtime can execute. <strong>Capability verification</strong> gathers scoped evidence that an implementation behaves as claimed under an exact contract, implementation version and platform. Contract and MCK test results provide evidence; they are not a formal proof, a claim about every possible input, or validation of business rules.</p><p><strong>Capability validation</strong> checks whether the selected runtime satisfies the requirements of a particular run, selected function and model revision. Its checking policy determines scope and timing: <strong>Whole model before running</strong> checks executable support up front; <strong>As code executes</strong> allows represented unsupported executable semantics to remain dormant and rejects them if reached. Both retain mandatory whole-source integrity, identity and resource checks and the complete selected input/output type closure before execution. Arguments, outputs, receipts and authorization still have their own validation rules.</p><p>Provider-authored capability claims remain distinct from verification evidence. An offline MCK adapter claim describes its exact test suite; it is not a live provider/session offer. A successful selected run does not expand the runtime capability set. Changing the checking policy changes when and where requirements are checked.</p><p>For compatibility, existing protocol keys, operation identifiers, profile and file paths, API names and strict/permissive wire values keep their historical spelling, including <code>admission</code>, <code>morphir/workbench/admission</code> and <code>AdmissionPolicy</code>. This vocabulary change adds no runtime behavior or capability advertisement.</p></section>
<section id="goal"><h2>Goal and scope</h2><p>A selected public Morphir function should run under an explicit permissive policy when unsupported code remains unused or is in a genuinely untaken branch. If evaluation reaches an unsupported operation, it fails deterministically. Strict remains the default. This document specifies proposed behavior and delivery gates; runtime behavior is unchanged by the planning task.</p><p>Initially qualify the bounded Scheme evaluator. This policy is independent of source language: Scheme, Elm or MoonBit source can participate only after producing accepted IR with a supported selected boundary. It does not imply generated JavaScript/Wasm/native execution, wider SDK support or any new platform capability.</p><p>Tree-shaking stays in <code>morphir-ukvn.6</code>. This policy retains original source and does not promise smaller artifacts, dead-code elimination or rollback of effects. Actual packaged-platform qualification remains in <code>morphir-etg2.15.5</code>.</p></section>
<section id="status"><h2>Evidence and completion</h2><p><label><input type="checkbox" checked disabled> Inspect the current engine, Scheme emitter/linker, terminal validation and immutable job paths.</label></p><p><label><input type="checkbox" checked disabled> Define strict/permissive semantics, selected boundaries, diagnostics and compatibility rules.</label></p><p><label><input type="checkbox" checked disabled> Store the reviewed HTML specification and six implementation tasks in Beads; sync the shared database and readable mirror.</label></p><p><label><input type="checkbox" checked disabled> Implement, independently qualify and merge the local capability-aware planner/evaluator.</label></p><p><label><input type="checkbox" disabled> Expose policy only on separately qualified standalone, native and HTTP integrations.</label></p><p>The planning milestone is <code>morphir-etg2.6</code>. Implementation belongs to <code>morphir-etg2.16</code>, so completing this design does not mark the feature implemented. Completion in the implementation plan follows qualified and merged deliveries.</p></section>
<section id="workflow"><h2>Capability validation and execution flow</h2><p><a href="../capability-vocabulary/diagram/runtime-capabilities.html">Explore the proposed policy workflow</a>. Current eager generation/linking are source-backed constraints; new policy routing and runtime traps are proposed.</p><figure style="margin:1rem 0"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," alt="Proposed policy workflow: mandatory whole-source checks, strict or selected-boundary planning, bounded execution, typed terminal validation and publication." style="width:100%;height:auto"><figcaption>New behavior requires a new exact contract and qualified capability. A picture is not execution evidence.</figcaption></figure><details><summary>Mermaid topology</summary><pre><code class="language-text">flowchart TD
  A[Capture source, calls and explicit policy] --> B[Whole-source framing, identities and limits]
  B --> C{Effective policy}
  C -->|strict default| D[Existing strict whole-library plan]
  C -->|explicit permissive| E[Selected public boundary closure]
  E --> F[Immutable bounded Scheme plan]
  F --> G{Operation reached?}
  G -->|supported| H[Execute normally]
  G -->|known unsupported| I[Failed: unsupported_reached]
  H --> J[Validate actual output and terminal inventory]
  I --> J
  J --> K[Publish against captured boundary context]
</code></pre></details></section>
<section id="policy"><h2>Policy and exact negotiation</h2><p>Use a closed internal policy type with <code>Strict</code> and <code>Permissive</code>. Proposed normative profile: <code>morphir/workbench/admission</code> <code>0.1.0-draft.1</code>. The effective policy is resolved once when the run is validated. An omitted field means strict only in a mapping that explicitly defines that omission. Unknown policy, profile, exact version, suffix, field or unsupported runtime selection rejects. Explicit permissive never silently falls back.</p><p>Separate the new Scheme plan from the existing generated-library <code>Plan</code> and <code>driver</code>. Do not invent an empty generated library to satisfy that API. The new plan retains original accepted IR, calls, authoritative public boundary context, policy/profile, evaluator/provider and identity. Replanning copies the accepted selection to a new plan identity; existing jobs remain unchanged.</p><pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;admission&quot;: {
    &quot;profile&quot;: &quot;morphir/workbench/admission&quot;,
    &quot;contractVersion&quot;: &quot;0.1.0-draft.1&quot;,
    &quot;policies&quot;: [
      &quot;strict&quot;,
      &quot;permissive&quot;
    ]
  }
}</code></pre><p>This descriptor fragment is a design proposal for a newly versioned host/native mapping. Existing closed draft.1 mappings reject it. Keep jobs draft.1 identities and receipt shapes when policy can remain wholly inside the immutable snapshot. A visible policy echo belongs in the new submission/plan projection, not an extra frozen receipt field.</p></section>
<section id="boundaries"><h2>Checks that always happen</h2><p>Parse and bound the entire source, including dormant code. Preserve accepted IR versions and grammar, envelope integrity, duplicate declaration/name/identity rejection, unambiguous lookup, resource accounting and source snapshot retention. Deferral is for represented unsupported semantics; it cannot recover malformed source or ambiguous authority.</p><p>Resolve every selected entry from actual IR and visibility, never a renderer-supplied manifest. Validate exact arity and tagged arguments. Validate the complete selected input and output type closure, including all variants, constructors, registry references and recursive relationships. Function-valued or unsupported selected boundaries reject even if the particular value is empty, None, a different constructor, a model error or a later failed/cancelled outcome. Unrelated declarations need bounded structural validation but need not all satisfy this provider's execution-support rules.</p><p>Type-only manifest extraction must be independent of body/code generation. Internal references do not grant public-call authorization. Native/external functions require existing host-authorized bindings; policy creates no filesystem, process, network, native callback or provider authority.</p><p>Use existing bounded codecs and runtime limits without increasing them. A new exact contract must state byte/unit/depth/node metrics and limits explicitly: whole-source and transport budgets, selected closure dimensions, argument/output budgets, optional-extension bytes, runtime steps/depth/frames and terminal/message bounds all apply. Dormant source and planned traps still count toward traversal and resource budgets. Resource or parser errors retain their original classes. Interpreter-only source/manifest traversal has its own explicit bounds; it must not borrow target artifact-size limits as a proxy. The existing typed Scheme limits are 1,000,000 steps, depth 128 and 100,000 frames; emission traversal currently limits depth to 256. File preparation and invocation share one active runtime budget. Thunks, nested calls and traps do not reset that budget or bypass cancellation.</p></section>
<section id="deferral"><h2>Deferral and reached failures</h2><p>Prepare definitions on demand, preserving their canonical identities and original source. Separately, lower known unsupported expressions to bounded traps at their original evaluation positions. Lazy declaration preparation solves an unused declaration; expression traps solve a hole or unsupported operation inside a loaded function's untaken branch. Static reachability alone cannot prove runtime branch selection.</p><p>Preserve the evaluator's order and laziness for each supported construct. Untaken conditional or short-circuit branches do not execute. Eager function arguments, bindings and pattern guards execute according to normal semantics, even when their result or variable name is unused. Do not catch arbitrary generator, parser, provider or resource failures and replace them with traps. Fully applied and/or have existing special short-circuit semantics; first-class and partially applied calls keep normal application semantics. Lambda creation does not execute its body; local let/letrec right-hand sides remain eager.</p><p>The unsupported inventory must explicitly cover constructor metadata and external fallback selection. Current Scheme loading eagerly registers all constructors with an arity cap of 256. Lazy value bodies alone do not solve an unrelated unsupported constructor. Define bounded on-demand metadata preparation without waiving the selected boundary closure. Generic signatures and alias cycles retain explicit supported/rejected status; finite named recursive ADTs require bounded complete closure. Native candidate order and arity checks remain deterministic. A chosen native implementation may leave an unsupported fallback dormant, but failure of that invoked native never triggers fallback.</p><pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;status&quot;: &quot;failed&quot;,
  &quot;failure&quot;: {
    &quot;stage&quot;: &quot;evaluate&quot;,
    &quot;code&quot;: &quot;execution.unsupported_reached&quot;,
    &quot;message&quot;: &quot;Unsupported operation reached&quot;
  }
}</code></pre><p>Reserve that code for actually reached, explicitly represented unsupported semantic operations. A missing authorized binding keeps its binding diagnostic; a wrong type, exhausted budget, malformed input, unavailable provider, crash or build failure keeps its own classification. SDK model errors remain enumerated model errors. Ordinary typed <code>Result.Err</code> remains returned data. MCK report <code>Unsupported</code> means a missing capability and is not this runtime terminal failure.</p><p>Bound human detail with existing terminal limits; no source text, paths, stack traces or secrets in stable diagnostics. Preserve completed effect accounting when a later operation fails. The policy supplies no rollback guarantee.</p></section>
<section id="outcomes"><h2>Validate actual results with the validated context</h2><p>Frozen draft.5 <code>validate-outcomes</code> revalidates the entire declaration graph before any terminal, including failed/cancelled. Do not send an original permissively deferred manifest through that operation and skip its rejection. Define a new policy-aware admission/outcome path that retains the sound selected-boundary closure and validates actual terminal evidence against that immutable context.</p><p>Reuse unchanged tagged-value and terminal-envelope codecs internally. Validate actual outputs, all supplied call identities in order, call count, extensions and independent cleanup. Do not claim the old whole-manifest operation qualified the deferred model. Cancellation and failed evidence never waive validation of the selected boundary. No runtime failure is encoded as a dummy value, new SDK model error or a successful Result.Err.</p></section>
<section id="capture"><h2>Capture policy through asynchronous execution</h2><p>Freeze source, entry, tagged arguments, accepted boundary context, exact policy/profile and evaluator/provider at submission. The same effective selection must reach the actual engine plan, job state, worker claim, execution and completion-time replanning/terminal validation. Resolve defaults once. A UI edit, reconnect, capability refresh, lease/claim or provider replacement cannot mutate an accepted job.</p><p>A new replan retains the selected policy with a new plan identity, or rejects if the new provider cannot honor it. Query, cancellation and release do not replan or execute again. Preserve existing once-only claim and uncertain-submission behavior. Independent fixed observations must inspect real captured policy and actual terminal results; controlled receipt/completion replay does not prove propagation through a worker.</p></section>
<section id="matrix"><h2>Acceptance matrix</h2><div role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison table; scroll horizontally on narrow screens" style="max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto"><table style="display:table;min-width:680px"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Strict</th><th>Explicit permissive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supported public call and fully supported library</td><td>Existing result.</td><td>Same typed result.</td></tr><tr><td>Unused unsupported declaration or dependency</td><td>Existing rejection stage and diagnostic.</td><td>Selected call may run; unused declaration is not initialized or executed.</td></tr><tr><td>Known unsupported expression in an untaken branch</td><td>Existing rejection.</td><td>Result from the taken branch.</td></tr><tr><td>Same unsupported expression is reached</td><td>Existing rejection.</td><td>Failed / evaluate / execution.unsupported_reached.</td></tr><tr><td>Eager binding or argument whose name/result is unused</td><td>Existing behavior.</td><td>Evaluate normally; a reached unsupported operation fails.</td></tr><tr><td>Private/missing entry, wrong arity, duplicate or ambiguous identity</td><td>Reject.</td><td>Reject before execution.</td></tr><tr><td>Malformed source/envelope/IR version or exhausted parse/source budget</td><td>Reject.</td><td>Reject, including malformed or oversized dormant code.</td></tr><tr><td>Unsupported selected input/output type closure</td><td>Reject.</td><td>Reject every unsupported declared alternative, even for empty List, None or an unselected constructor.</td></tr><tr><td>Missing reference in selected custom type, recursive boundary or private constructor</td><td>Existing declaration validation.</td><td>Complete selected closure required; reuse bounded recursive validation, never waive it based on an actual value.</td></tr><tr><td>Native/external body without a supported binding</td><td>Existing failure.</td><td>May remain dormant. Reaching a missing binding fails with its own binding diagnostic; no host capability is created. A represented unsupported operation uses the reserved reached-unsupported code.</td></tr><tr><td>SDK model error or ordinary Result.Err</td><td>Existing distinct outcome.</td><td>Same distinct validated outcome, never a sentinel for unsupported execution.</td></tr><tr><td>Malformed/wrong/oversized returned value or terminal inventory</td><td>Reject publication.</td><td>Reject publication against the captured validated selected context.</td></tr><tr><td>Cancellation, provider failure, budget exhaustion and cleanup failure</td><td>Existing classifications.</td><td>Preserve classifications, call inventory, extensions and independent primary/cleanup causes.</td></tr><tr><td>Provider/runtime change, restored draft, claim or replan</td><td>Existing strict selection.</td><td>Preserve accepted selection or reject a new incompatible plan. Never silently downgrade.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="qualification"><h2>Upstream contract and qualification</h2><p>Publish the normative policy and fixed expectations back to <code>finos/morphir</code>. Propose an isolated exact offline MCK version, currently the next available <code>0.1.0-draft.11</code>, with the narrow JSON-only operation <code>run-selected-scheme</code> for Task 1. Its immutable fixture kit provides evidence only for selected public boundary planning and execution beside unused unsupported declarations, including observed decode/plan/evaluate refusals. It does not qualify the full policy, runtime branch semantics, terminal inventory, a provider or UI capability. Task 2 uses exact <code>0.1.0-draft.12</code> and <code>run-capability-policy</code> for the applicable Library-only Scheme policy semantics; it never modifies frozen draft.11. The 182 fixed cases separate 87 actual-execution observations from 95 supplied-completion validation observations. The latter validate retained context without executing the model, authenticating a job or establishing actual cancellation. Confirm every next free exact version at implementation time; do not repurpose another effort's version. Transport version, policy profile, evaluator capability and host/native mapping version are separate identities.</p><p>The shared Rust runner/checker owns corpus validation, bounded input-only dispatch, fixed expected observations, comparisons, corpus hashes, ordered case/format/operation inventory and report rechecking. Parent-owned models and expected results are independent of the adapter. Keep every frozen draft unchanged. No MoonBit or TypeScript verdict engine, expected-result dispatch, clock-based settling or adapter-supplied public authority.</p><p>Task 1 may establish evidence for an internal focused tracer, but public capability advertisement waits for Task 2's complete applicable semantics corpus and independent report checks. Initial local lanes are named explicitly and tested in debug/release. JSON transport may contain explicitly identified JSON or Ion-text arguments; that does not establish general Ion binary/reverse-encoding qualification.</p><p>Native and HTTP adoption need new exact closed mappings and actual end-to-end evidence. Old native/HTTP draft.1 stay strict and reject added members. The existing Rust protocol v1 and its <code>jobs:false</code> regression remain unchanged. A real TypeScript gateway/provider test establishes that host's behavior only. Generated backend execution and each packaged OS/architecture need their own implementation and qualification before advertisement.</p></section>
<section id="resources"><h2>Measured budgets and SDK limits</h2><p>Source, selected-type, value and encoded-byte limits and interpreter fuel/depth/frame budgets remain mandatory. They do not establish a hard process-memory or CPU quota over opaque SDK internals. SDK operations such as String.repeat may allocate before result validation. Allocation/cancellation hardening is tracked separately in <code>morphir-be5t</code>. Worker deadlines and process controls have their own scope.</p><p>Retained constructor metadata above arity 256 is a preparation resource refusal, including dormant branches; unrelated constructors remain pruned. Actual Scheme Decimal results follow the existing SDK coefficient/exponent normalization law, preserving numeric value without a cohort/scale guarantee. Supplied completion validation performs no arithmetic and retains accepted wire representation. Accepted implementation decision: <code>morphir-8n2g</code>.</p></section>
<section id="ui"><h2>Workbench behavior</h2><p>Reuse existing evaluation controls, reusable editors, typed fields/JSON/Ion inputs and terminal/diagnostic bottom panel. Keep the policy choice compact and explicit, with strict selected by default. Dynamic Scheme and worksheet mode remain separate from typed capability validation as code executes. Preserve original IR and generation diagnostics when typed Scheme evaluation can be supported independently of generation.</p><p>Scope capability to the actual runtime, entry and boundary. Before submission, a runtime or restored draft that cannot honor the requested selection shows an incompatibility reason and requires an explicit compatible choice. Never silently change policy. A running job displays its captured selection and retains it after edits. Existing results, focus/navigation, cancellation, stale-result protection and code-area space remain covered by real browser and Proton acceptance.</p></section>
<section id="delivery"><h2>Implementation sequence</h2><p><a href="../beads-plans/morphir-etg2.16.html">Six-slice implementation plan</a>. Tasks 3 and 4 can proceed after Task 2; native and HTTP follow both. The planning task can close after the design, implementation records, review and sync are complete. The implementation epic stays open. Merge/publication follow the existing maintainer approval rules.</p><div role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison table; scroll horizontally on narrow screens" style="max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto"><table style="display:table;min-width:680px"><thead><tr><th>Slice</th><th>Blocked by</th><th>Delivery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Run a supported function alongside unsupported code</td><td>Planning complete</td><td>A supported public Int42 entry can be planned and evaluated beside a valid but unsupported unused declaration under an explicit private permissive selection. Omitted and explicit strict keep their existing behavior. This is the first internal tracer; no public runtime capability is advertised yet.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Report unsupported code only when execution reaches it</td><td>Task 1</td><td>The same known unsupported expression succeeds when its branch is not evaluated and fails with failed/evaluate/execution.unsupported_reached when reached. Supported results remain fully typed, and the complete local policy corpus passes independent checking before the local capability is exposed.</td></tr><tr><td>3. Retain the chosen runtime and checking policy for each job</td><td>Task 2</td><td>A submitted policy, source, selected entry, arguments, boundary context and evaluator/provider selection are captured once. UI edits, reconnect, claim and replan cannot silently switch an accepted job to another policy or runtime.</td></tr><tr><td>4. Expose capability checks in the standalone Workbench</td><td>Task 2</td><td>Explorer and Try Morphir offer an explicit policy choice for a selected public function only when the local evaluator supports it. A known unsupported operation in an untaken branch can evaluate; reaching it produces the existing terminal diagnostic presentation.</td></tr><tr><td>5. Carry capability checks through desktop jobs</td><td>Task 3, Task 4</td><td>A qualified native Proton session can explicitly negotiate and execute a permissive job through its owned worker. Legacy native sessions remain strict and reject new fields.</td></tr><tr><td>6. Carry capability checks through connected jobs</td><td>Task 3, Task 4</td><td>A policy-aware authenticated HTTP host can advertise and honor an explicit permissive submission. Strict-only and legacy connected hosts continue to expose their existing capabilities without accepting new policy fields.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>Source evidence and limits</h2><div role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison table; scroll horizontally on narrow screens" style="max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto"><table style="display:table;min-width:680px"><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Pinned observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MoonBit baseline</td><td>4cb4c6decdf29f31893a2d3364929a3f1fe9f47f, merged PR #117.</td></tr><tr><td>Parent baseline</td><td>a14e7c62821b8d98926dc35d4946d06af7075dd7, merged PR #1055.</td></tr><tr><td>Generation before selected validation</td><td>pkgs/morphir-engine/execution/execution.mbt:119-155; apps/morphir-workbench/typed_evaluation.mbt:4-7, 79-113.</td></tr><tr><td>Whole-file linking and lowering</td><td>pkgs/morphir-scheme/backend/native.mbt:69-105, 192-220; emit.mbt:150-195, 387, 488-511.</td></tr><tr><td>Boundary support and output validation</td><td>pkgs/morphir-moonbit/invocation_codecs.mbt:341-368; pkgs/morphir-execution/outcomes.mbt:407-438.</td></tr><tr><td>Captured jobs</td><td>pkgs/morphir-engine/execution/jobs/session.mbt:19-26, 59-110, 140-148; browser/job-session.ts:8, 60-68, 121-129.</td></tr><tr><td>Frozen complete-manifest requirement</td><td>finos/morphir spec/workbench/draft.3/README.md:49-71, draft.4/README.md:34-57, draft.5/README.md:33-66.</td></tr><tr><td>Closed live mappings and evidence limits</td><td>finos/morphir spec/workbench/jobs/draft.1/README.md:16-40; host/draft.1/README.md:77-110; native/draft.1/README.md:88-124; draft.10/README.md:197-217.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a href="../permissive-admission/runtime-audit.md">Runtime audit</a> and <a href="../permissive-admission/contract-audit.md">shared-contract audit</a> retain detailed source citations. Source and module paths are implementation pointers, not public protocol names. This is a source-backed proposal; no runtime, protocol or platform support is introduced by this HTML document.</p></section></main>
